If you have any questions, just comment below

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.040
and let me know.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.280
Are you a individual contributor

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.920
that is curious about engineering leadership

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.400
or you wanna know what it takes

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.600
to become a successful engineering manager?

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.720
As I reflected back in my career,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.040
there were a few things that I kind of wish I knew

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.700
before I made the transition

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.240
to becoming an engineering manager.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.880
So in this video, I'm going to cover it all.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.020
So first of all, I wanna say

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.680
that I was quite happy with the move,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.680
but I really want you to know all these things

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.380
so you can make the best decision for yourself.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.880
So let's get started.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.140
So the first thing that I experienced as a transition

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.360
from being a developer to an engineering manager

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.520
was that you do feel like an outsider to your very own team.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.620
Now you have a lot more power, right?

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.720
Being a leader, you can basically decide

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.760
who you get to hire within the team, who gets promoted.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:13.360
You know all the salaries of your team members.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.300
So you're not going to look at your team

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.280
the same way as you did before.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.120
So you start feeling like an outsider

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.200
to your very own team.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.980
That is the first thing you will experience

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.480
as you make the transition, or at least I did.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.140
Because you have a lot more control and power,

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.800
you are now in charge of making the decisions.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.600
Your team is going to look up to you as a leader.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.080
If they don't know what to do,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.120
they are going to look up to you for that.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.320
So that is a bit of a shift and a mindset shift

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.080
that you need to go through as you make the transition.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.040
So the percentage of time that you will spend coding

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.000
will drastically and significantly reduce

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.800
as a engineering manager.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.760
A lot of people have this misconception

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.440
that as you transition from being a developer

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.360
to an engineering manager, it is basically a promotion.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.760
It is the same role, maybe with a little bit more

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.880
of people managing responsibilities.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.920
Well, if you want to be a mediocre engineering manager,

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.720
then sure, you can do that,

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.160
but don't spend a lot of time coding.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.640
Because again, you have a lot more responsibility

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.320
as an engineering manager.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.720
The first goal should be to grow the people on your team,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.040
make sure that they are set up for success,

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.520
make sure you are reviewing their performance,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.300
giving constructive feedback, managing stakeholders,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.520
doing roadmap planning for the future.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.260
And there is lots more that you need to do

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.480
to make sure that you are setting up your team

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.880
for success as an engineering leader.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.940
They are looking up to you for leadership.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.480
So if you spend a lot of time coding behind the computer,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.800
then you will necessarily will not have time

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.360
to focus on your other important responsibilities,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.280
and this will not make you a successful engineering manager.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.120
So you might be wondering, well, if I'm not gonna code,

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.560
then how am I called an engineering manager?

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.280
Will I not just be a manager then?

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.440
Well, here's the thing.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.880
You still have a lot of technical expertise

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.500
that got you to where you were, right?

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.760
But when you take on an engineering manager position,

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.480
it's a completely different role.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.360
You can still use your strengths from being a developer,

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.440
and you should definitely be involved

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.760
in knowing all the architectural decisions,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.480
to making sure that your team has really good standards

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.680
technically, make sure there are good code review processes,

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.760
make sure that you understand

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.200
all the architectural decisions being made,

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.080
and your team is not just thinking

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.360
just for that specific module that you're working on,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.480
but they're also thinking about the scalability as well,

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:35.320
right?

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.320
So your experience as a developer

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.680
is going to definitely help you

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.280
when you transition to become an engineering manager.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.640
But again, what got you here will not necessarily get you

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.000
to where you wanna go

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.200
being a successful engineering manager.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.340
So make sure that you keep this in mind as you transition.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.480
So again, should you code?

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.480
Well, it totally depends on your team's responsibilities.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.480
If you have everything else under control,

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.840
if you do wanna pick up a task here and there,

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:07.200
just to make sure you understand the code base

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.400
a little bit more,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.400
you are able to give feedback to your team,

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:13.600
and you're not just a manager managing,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.540
you also are involved,

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.280
and they feel that you are involved as well,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.280
then you can definitely do that.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.080
But again, make sure that the main focus of your position,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.120
of your role is not to code.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.440
It's all these other things,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.940
including maybe a little bit of coding.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.120
So again, make sure you don't spend a lot of time

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:33.960
doing that.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.280
You will feel a therapist many times

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.480
as you deal with people.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.600
So the people on your team are going to come to you

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.400
with all sorts of problems.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.520
Their performance is not necessarily tied

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.080
to their day-to-day job,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.020
but also what's going on personally,

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.260
what's going on outside of work as well.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.000
So sometimes you might feel

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.800
like you know a lot more about them personally as well

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.840
in order to make sure you connect with them professionally.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.920
And you don't necessarily need to do that,

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.440
but I guess being a good engineering manager,

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.980
you need to care about the people that you work for.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.880
Well, you will feel like a therapist at times

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.840
because you will spend a lot of time talking

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.120
about their thoughts and feelings,

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.560
but it's also important that you start drawing boundaries

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.420
because people are going through maybe a lot of stuff.

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:26.160
So if they need to talk to someone professional

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:27.300
other than you,

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.140
then definitely advise them of that as well.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:32.580
But you will feel at times that it's necessary

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:33.560
to talk to them,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:34.880
not just about work stuff,

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.580
but also about personal stuff.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.760
So make sure that you keep it all confidential

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.040
because they think of you as a leader and they trust you

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:44.480
and that's why they're sharing all this information with you.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:45.960
So make sure that you do that.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.760
For an example, there was a developer who I was managing

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.280
and they clearly were not giving their 100%.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.480
And I personally felt I could see that they were not,

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.080
even though they were always on top of their performance,

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.160
in this specific case,

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.880
they were not necessarily doing that.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.940
Their performance was not great anymore.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.440
So I got really curious to just check in

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.000
and ask them what's impacting them.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.780
And it turns out there was something personal going on.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.860
Without getting into too many details,

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.440
I was able to navigate and guide them

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:18.960
in terms of how they can manage

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:20.660
their personal and professional life.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.240
And I also asked them to maybe take a day off,

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:24.080
a personal day,

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.500
just to make sure that they take care of that

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.320
before they can focus on work.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.520
The next day they came back

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.940
and they were able to really recharge

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.220
and really take the time off

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.720
and deal with whatever they had to deal with personally

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:39.880
to be able to show up at work and give their 100%.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.440
So again, this is important that you have that empathy

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.880
and you treat people as humans

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.240
and not just resources when you are an engineering manager.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.440
Being an engineering manager can be lonely at times.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:56.760
Like I mentioned that you will not feel like

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.160
the same team member anymore

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.560
as you did before when you are an IC.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:05.240
And as you become an engineering manager,

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.560
you cannot necessarily talk to the developers on your team

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:10.720
about the problems that are going on in the company

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.040
because as a leader,

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.760
you need to shield them from that.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.080
You need to make sure that they feel comfortable

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.200
talking to you about things

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.620
and they feel like their position is in a good spot.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.640
If you talk to them only about the problems that are there

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:27.200
or whatever is going on in your mind,

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.840
then they might not feel as confident about the team

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:31.160
and the company that they work at.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.400
So make sure that you shield them of that,

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.600
but also find a support system for yourself

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.020
and other engineering leaders

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.560
that you can talk to about problems as well.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.440
So maybe find a mentor, take care of your own mental health

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.600
and make sure that you have a good support system

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.680
so that you can talk about problems

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:54.240
or and come up with solutions as well.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.840
The direct reports are also going to share something

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.680
personal and private with you

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.600
and you will not be able to share that with anyone else

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.580
and you need to keep it confidential, right?

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:06.520
Because that is their personal information.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.120
There's a lot that you will keep to yourself

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.600
and which will also make engineering leadership

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.800
a little bit lonely, but it doesn't have to be.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.680
You can find a support system, you can join a community,

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.300
you can talk to other people and their leaders

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.280
going through the same journey as you

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.120
so that you don't feel alone

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.280
because it can definitely feel lonely at times.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:30.680
I was also socially exhausted being in a lot of meetings

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:34.640
as I transitioned from being a developer to a manager.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.400
Now, I was booked in a lot of back-to-back meetings

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.900
because everyone wanted to get a piece of me,

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:45.440
everyone had to pick my brain on things

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:48.740
and I had to make a lot of decisions just to make sure

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:51.000
that my team is on the right path.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.480
But it doesn't have to be,

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.540
you don't have to be socially exhausted.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:57.920
So you need to start planning your week a little bit better,

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.320
kind of figure out what the focus of the week is,

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.240
what your priorities are of that week

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:05.680
and accordingly push the things that are not that important

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.340
to the next week and try to say no as well,

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.680
which we'll cover later in this specific video,

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.880
but you will be socially exhausted

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.680
as you will be in a lot more meetings than you did

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:16.920
being a developer.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.920
As an engineering manager, you will shield the team

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:21.760
from unnecessary meetings,

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:23.760
but that also means you are going to accept

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.860
a lot more meetings because you wanna make sure

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.680
that you have things under control.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.120
So you will be socially exhausted,

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:31.560
so make sure that you spend some time,

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.800
take some time in the morning or in the afternoon

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.480
and book some focus time for yourself in your calendar

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:37.800
that no one gets to book over

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.280
so that we can get some meet time

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.880
and are also able to get some work done as well.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.000
But that is a reality.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.040
You will feel socially exhausted at first

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.160
without proper systems in place.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.160
You are going to find a bit difficult to say no at first.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:52.560
Now, unless you were a leader before

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.480
and you are already good at that, then please ignore this.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.840
But if you're not, then it is definitely going to be

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.360
a bit difficult for you to say no

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.400
because everyone would need a piece of your team.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.360
They would all come to you for priorities,

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.760
their priorities that they need to work on,

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.920
and you need to figure out how you can

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.040
position those priorities along with current priorities

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:14.560
that your team is working on.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.920
If you say yes a lot, your team is going to be burnt out.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.320
So make sure that you don't say yes to everyone.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:25.520
A strategy that I transitioned was,

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:27.840
instead of saying no, you can talk to them

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:30.480
about the priorities that you do have

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.680
and accordingly prioritize their stuff

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.040
maybe in the next quarter

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.120
or maybe a month from now and so on.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:39.160
So you're not necessarily saying no,

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:43.320
you are saying not now, let's do this at this specific day.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.040
So again, but you're going to have it

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:48.560
a bit of a difficult time initially to say no

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:51.000
and make sure that you know what your priorities are.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.200
As you transition to being a manager,

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.680
you are going to manage a team

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:57.960
that will have different personalities.

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:00.560
So for example, some might be more extroverted

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:01.880
and will speak up a lot more

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:04.520
but others are not as extroverted

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:06.000
and they don't speak up

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.680
and maybe need an opportunity to do so.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:13.080
So for example, extroverted folks are going to talk

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.080
as they think and they will keep talking.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:18.800
Whereas introverted folks will need some time

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:21.440
to think first and then talk about anything.

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:24.060
So you really need to make sure that you're balancing

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:26.600
both personality types in a specific team

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:27.780
so that you're not just listening

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:31.080
to one specific personality type as you make decisions.

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:33.080
You need to balance it out to make sure

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:36.840
that you are asking questions

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.020
and making sure that you're including everyone

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:41.840
in the meeting, making sure that you have organized

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:44.980
the meeting in such a way that everyone can speak up

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:46.560
and give their thoughts.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:50.580
Maybe you can ask someone about what they are thinking

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:51.420
and so on.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:52.800
But again, you are going to deal

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:54.600
with a lot of different personality types.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.040
So you need to make sure you need to balance them all.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:59.400
Otherwise you're only going to lean onto the person

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.360
that is the loudest and you really don't want to do that

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:02.800
as an engineering manager.

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:04.920
You want to create a transparent culture

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:08.000
and where people are happier to work for you.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:11.400
They are excited in the morning to come work for you

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.840
for the team and you are going to be a huge part of that.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:16.720
That's why it's important for you to make sure

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:19.480
that you take this into account.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:23.680
What got you here will not necessarily get you further.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:26.240
As a developer, it's your job to make sure

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.080
that your skills are up to date, right?

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:32.200
So if it is a specific technology that you work in your team,

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:33.780
for example, React, how do you make sure

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.520
that the performance of the app is really good

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:38.760
or how do things work with server components

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.060
and so on, right?

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:43.600
Now, React is important as a developer

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:46.120
because that's the technology that your team is using.

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.160
But when you transition to be a manager,

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.480
you shouldn't be focusing on making sure

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.760
that you have up-to-date React skills.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:57.160
You need to be focused on how can you be the best manager

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:58.560
that you can be?

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:00.480
And those skills are very different

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.320
than being a developer skills.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:03.800
So make sure you're spending time

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:06.260
on the development of your skills as well,

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:08.960
such as how do you give constructive feedback?

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:10.560
How do you promote someone?

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:13.720
How do you do performance reviews and so on?

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.040
Sometimes you are going to disappoint people

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:19.880
because their promotion did not get accepted.

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:24.160
And even though you think that they deserve to get promoted,

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:28.120
you need to be in a position to put them forward

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.720
in the best possible way.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.740
And those are skills that you need to learn

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:34.960
which you didn't necessarily knew about

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:36.440
when you were a developer.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.400
So again, there are a lot more skills

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:41.000
that you need to learn being an engineering manager

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:43.440
that you won't necessarily know as a developer.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:45.840
So make sure you keep learning

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:49.240
and figure out how others are doing it in the company.

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:51.240
Are there specific strategies and frameworks

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.640
that you can use to make sure

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.920
that you're giving feedback the right way?

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:57.460
Or if someone is causing any troubles in the team,

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:59.720
then make sure they are aware of that

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:02.280
without making them feel too bad about it.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.160
There's so many things that you will deal with

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:05.600
as an engineering manager

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.520
that you didn't necessarily deal with before.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:10.680
So make sure that you are on top of your personal skills

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:14.460
as well and your reading books, eBooks, and so on,

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:17.280
just to make sure that you have control of everything.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.800
And again, this makes the journey fun for you as well

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:23.400
because you're not just stuck with the skills

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.700
that you transitioned with.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:28.500
You also are always improving and growing

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:32.200
and experimenting with the team that you're working with

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.880
so that your team becomes a really high-performing team.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:40.160
Unfortunately, not every day is going to be a good day

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:41.760
being a manager.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.480
Sometimes you have to give really difficult feedback

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.240
to your team or a specific team member

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:50.040
and their response to that might be really emotional

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:52.440
because the feedback is really difficult.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.720
And because of that, your day is going to be impacted.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.640
You are going to spend hours talking to someone

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.820
just to make sure that they understand the impact

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:02.840
they're possibly having on the team.

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:06.040
And again, that can be very exhausting.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:08.600
Sometimes you want to get someone promoted,

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:11.920
but their promotion is not going to get accepted

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:13.600
or they want more salary

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:17.760
and you are unable to get it approved for whatever reason.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.520
You are going to have a bad day because of that

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:24.260
because it is going to be really emotional for you.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:28.080
So being an engineering manager can be very difficult

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:29.740
because you are dealing with people

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:32.080
and people are humans, not just computers.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:34.320
When you are a developer, you're writing code

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.180
and you're just dealing with computers,

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.960
you're obviously dealing with team dynamics

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.800
and communication and whatnot.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:42.400
But being a manager, that is in the forefront.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:43.760
That's why you really need to make sure

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.880
that you're digging care of your mental health

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:49.060
as you transition because it's not just

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:50.860
your team's mental health that's important,

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:54.440
it's also yours because if yours will start to get affected,

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:57.700
then the team is going to experience the same as well.

00:16:57.700 --> 00:17:00.240
It's the same analogy that when you're in an airplane,

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:03.320
make sure that you're wearing an oxygen mask as well

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:05.080
before you give it to other people.

00:17:05.080 --> 00:17:06.980
It's the same thing being a manager

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:10.400
that you are always in service of other people,

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:12.760
but make sure that you also take care of your mental health

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:14.280
and your personal needs as well.

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:16.000
And if you do need to take the time off,

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.920
then please do so because that will have an impact

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:19.880
on others too.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:23.040
So again, make sure you take care of yourself.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.520
What if you don't like being an engineering manager

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:27.480
and you want to go back to being a developer,

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:29.800
you really like coding and now you have to deal

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:31.480
with all these different things

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:33.380
that you don't necessarily want to deal with

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:36.380
or you had a misunderstanding of the position

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:39.600
and I want to say that if you do give it a shot,

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.200
let's say there's an opportunity available in your team

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:44.840
or in a company and you decided to go for it

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:47.360
and six months later or a year later, you are like,

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:49.720
you know what, I love being a manager,

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:53.880
but I love being a developer as well, so I want to go back.

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:56.260
It's completely okay for you to go back.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:58.360
In fact, I would highly recommend it.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:00.060
It's important to keep going back and forth

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.280
between being a manager and being a developer

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:06.520
so that you are really appreciative of both positions

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:08.600
because a lot of times when you're a developer,

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:10.960
you don't necessarily know what an engineering manager

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.120
has to go through

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:15.200
because they don't necessarily share everything with the team

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.520
because they're supposed to shield the team, right?

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:20.680
So being a manager and then switching to a developer,

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:23.280
you are going to build a lot more empathy as well,

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:26.960
but there's nothing wrong in switching back and forth.

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:28.400
In fact, it's totally recommended

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:30.140
because if you think about it,

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:32.160
in your career throughout your life,

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:34.880
if you want to stick to just being one position,

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.640
then it's probably not going to be as challenging

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:39.480
by switching back and forth,

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:41.280
you're learning a lot more skills

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:43.800
that will be helpful in either way.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:46.600
So again, if you really want to switch, go for it,

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.160
but I would highly recommend you

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:50.080
to also give it a shot to see

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:51.880
if you really like being an engineering manager

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:54.560
and making sure that your skills are up to date

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.240
and you are always learning and growing

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:59.080
because as a developer, you do that,

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.120
so do the same when you transition to being a manager.

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:05.560
Well, I talked a lot about what it was like

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:07.320
when I transitioned from being a developer

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:08.700
to an engineering manager.

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:10.080
I know you might be wondering,

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:13.120
is it even worth it to become an engineering manager?

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:14.740
Well, I would say yes,

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:17.960
it's really rewarding seeing the growth of people

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:20.960
and the impact that you are going to have on folks.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:22.760
I have worked with so many different people

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.520
and when I do give them feedback

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.400
or I provide suggestions and insights

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:29.160
based on my experience to help them grow,

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:31.760
it is awesome to see that they work on those

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:35.080
and they grow because of you as well.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:38.720
So the impact is going to be huge on other people's career.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:42.280
So again, being an engineering manager is really rewarding.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:45.400
The other thing is you will have a lot more influence

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:49.260
and the impact is really growing to grow massively

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:51.120
in a company because your focus

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:53.040
is not just going to be your team,

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:55.120
but your impact is going to be really visible

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.160
across the org as well, right?

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:59.880
So being a developer, you only think about your team,

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:01.780
but being an engineering manager,

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:05.360
your influence and your impact is going to grow a lot more,

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.520
which won't necessarily be possible being a developer.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.040
By being an engineering manager,

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.260
you get to control or have a say on the roadmap

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.360
on what your team gets to work on.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.400
So based on the goals of the company,

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.960
you get to decide what's important for the team.

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:22.800
The problems that you will solve as an engineering manager

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:24.960
will be at a larger scale

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:27.120
and it won't be at a smaller scale anymore.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:29.520
So again, you are going to be solving bigger problems

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:31.280
that are important for the company

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:35.000
for not just your one team, but several teams.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:38.480
So your impact is going to be a lot more

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:40.720
if you take advantage of it, being a manager.

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:43.120
If teams are not working together,

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:46.120
you get to be the one to make sure

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:48.520
that they talk to each other.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.480
They have the same common understanding.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:53.180
You become the decision maker,

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:56.460
but also someone who comes and resolves problems.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.600
And it sometimes is going to be hard

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:01.640
because misunderstandings can happen,

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:04.640
but as an engineering manager, you are the leader.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.520
So they are going to look up to you

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:08.360
for solving all their problems.

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.280
And even though you don't necessarily need

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:12.160
to solve the problem on your own,

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.340
you can make sure that the teams get together

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:16.920
and talk it out and facilitate the meetings

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.440
so that problems get resolved.

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:20.800
So the problems that you'll be solving

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:23.760
won't just be your own problem

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:26.320
as you were when you were a developer,

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:28.900
but you get to control and you get to work

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:30.780
on a lot more problems because of it.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.240
So I would say that being an engineering manager

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:35.080
is really rewarding.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:37.840
If there is an opportunity in your organization

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:40.640
or your team where you get to be a leader,

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:43.280
I would highly recommend you to take up that position

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:45.440
and learn the skills necessary

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:47.520
to become a engineering manager.

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:48.760
Now, having said that,

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:51.600
I hope this video was really useful for you.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.780
I really wanted to make sure that I cover all my feelings

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:58.800
that I went through from being AIC to a manager.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:01.400
And before you take up the position,

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.800
I want you to know what you need to be aware of

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:05.840
as you go through so you don't feel alone.

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:09.240
Software career paths, career ladders, you name it.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:11.520
There are so many different names for the same thing.

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:13.600
It is extremely important as a developer

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:15.960
to know what career path is right for you

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:18.200
and what are the different levels you can go to

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:20.960
as you grow in your career.

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.120
There are different titles in the industry

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:25.600
and every title is so confusing.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:27.600
It's hard to know whether you should become

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:30.840
an engineering manager or a staff developer

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:32.160
or a technical lead.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:34.680
So we will be talking about titles

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:36.760
and how they can be so confusing

00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:39.400
and what are some things that you should be aware of

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:40.440
in tech.

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:43.720
So as a developer, there are typically two career paths

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:46.080
that you can take and there are different levels

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:47.340
in different companies.

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:49.740
Now, there is a diagram right in front of us

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:52.380
and I'm just showing you this diagram

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:54.800
just to give you an understanding of the different levels

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:57.240
and what these fancy titles are like

00:22:57.240 --> 00:22:59.520
so you get a really good understanding

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:02.040
of what every level is expected to do

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:03.480
and what to know about it.

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:05.760
So for example, if you are a developer,

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:06.680
there are two paths.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:09.400
One is called the individual contributor path

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:12.160
and the other one is the leadership track.

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:16.520
Now, a lot of times you will find that an IC path

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:21.080
has not that many levels compared to a leadership path

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:24.560
and that is why a lot of developers end up becoming managers

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:27.880
because they don't know how else to grow

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:30.360
unless they go into a leadership track

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.000
but it doesn't necessarily have to.

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:34.480
You need to find the right company and the team

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:36.300
where these levels are properly defined

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.640
so you can keep growing

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:40.400
even after you reach a specific level.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:42.800
So for example, as a developer,

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:46.080
you can first become a entry-level developer,

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.880
then you join to an intermediate developer

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:51.000
and then you go to a senior developer

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.880
but whereas if you are already a senior developer

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:55.600
or even a high intermediate,

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:58.700
you can become a technical lead, a staff dev

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:00.440
or a engineering manager.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.040
I have some good news.

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:04.400
I have been working on a course to take you

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:07.160
from a developer to a engineering manager

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:11.120
and make you a very successful engineering manager.

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:14.040
So if you're interested, add yourself to the wait list.

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:16.480
This course will cover everything that we have talked about

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:19.120
in this specific engineering manager series

00:24:19.120 --> 00:24:22.240
but there's a lot more that I couldn't cover in this series

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:24.240
that I will cover in the course.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.080
So please feel free to check it out

00:24:26.080 --> 00:24:28.060
and take a look in the description below.

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:30.000
Now it's a very common thing

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.240
that there would be three different levels

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:33.600
in a developer titles.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:36.520
For example, there would be an entry-level developer,

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:39.640
an intermediate developer and a senior developer

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:41.760
and a senior is supposed to take on

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.620
a lot more leadership responsibilities and tasks

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:48.800
compared to an intermediate or an entry-level developer

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:53.520
and a senior developer is also maybe asked to mentor

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:55.520
maybe more entry-level devs

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:57.980
or maybe devs more junior to them

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:00.720
just so that they start practicing

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:02.920
their leadership responsibilities as well.

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:05.600
So being senior doesn't just mean that you write code

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:07.780
but you also need to make sure that you're mentoring others

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:10.140
and helping improve your processes,

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.840
your team processes and so on as well.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.080
But the minute you become a senior developer,

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:17.080
it does open up two paths for you.

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:19.520
One is that you can become a technical lead,

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:21.000
a staff developer.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.960
After that, you can become a solution architect.

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:27.720
Now again, it completely depends in every company

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:30.880
that after a senior developer, you can become an architect.

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:33.360
And what is the difference between an architect

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:36.160
and a staff developer or a tech lead?

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:38.640
Well, an architect is mainly responsible

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:40.520
for architecting the different apps.

00:25:40.520 --> 00:25:43.200
They are responsible for maybe growing the team.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.320
An architect really takes a look

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:47.960
at the company's architecture holistically

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:49.960
and recommends the best practices

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:52.840
and makes sure that the architecture makes sense.

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:54.880
And you might even have heard the joke

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:57.480
that an architect does draw a lot of boxes,

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:01.240
which is not wrong, but an architect does a lot more

00:26:01.240 --> 00:26:03.400
and they think about the company holistically,

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:06.440
the company architecture holistically technically.

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:09.960
Then a senior developer could also become a technical lead

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:13.960
or a staff dev and a tech lead would essentially

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:16.880
be more responsible for leading

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:21.440
all the technical responsibilities of a developer,

00:26:21.440 --> 00:26:22.880
for example.

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:25.560
But depending on the company, after a tech lead,

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:28.120
you can become a principal developer

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:31.160
or a distinguished engineer or a fellow.

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:32.920
Now again, these are different levels

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:35.720
and the responsibilities keep growing.

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:40.040
But again, and the scale at which you do these things

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:41.320
are also different.

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:43.480
So for example, a distinguished engineer

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:46.240
or a principal developer or a fellow role

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:48.760
may not be available in more smaller

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:50.240
or medium-sized companies

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.440
because there's not a need for it.

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:56.280
But in bigger companies such as the Fortune 500 companies,

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:58.280
you will more likely see these roles

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:01.320
just so that people, when they reach a specific level,

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:02.400
they're still able to grow

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:04.360
and they have something to strive for

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:06.040
and they don't just leave the company.

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:08.320
So as a developer, you can take the IC path

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:09.840
and this is what your path will look like.

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:12.320
So how does a leadership path look like?

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:14.760
Well, let's say you are a senior dev

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:17.280
or a tech lead or a solution architect

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:22.080
and you can basically go to becoming a engineering manager.

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:23.840
So you might be wondering,

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:25.720
what's the difference between a tech lead

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:27.920
and an engineering manager?

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:30.600
Well, or a staff developer and engineering manager.

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:33.240
Now, tech lead and staff developer

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.640
can be completely different responsibilities and roles

00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:37.120
and most likely they are,

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.280
but a lot of companies also have them

00:27:39.280 --> 00:27:40.560
more or less the same.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.080
So let's talk about the difference between a tech lead

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:44.160
and an engineering manager.

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:45.600
You can think of a tech lead

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:48.440
as a technical partner to an engineering manager.

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:50.880
An engineering manager is responsible

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:53.320
for the leadership of the team.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:55.320
They are responsible for making sure

00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:58.320
their proper processes, they're managing the people,

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:01.080
they are understanding the organization strategy

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:04.080
and making sure that the technical strategy is also intact.

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:06.800
But who is responsible for executing it?

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:07.680
It's the tech lead.

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:09.040
A tech lead is responsible

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:11.840
for all the technical aspect of things.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:14.440
Having said that, sometimes a tech lead

00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:17.600
is in fact the engineering manager in specific companies.

00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:20.480
But a tech lead more or less would be responsible

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:23.800
for executing the technical vision of the company

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:26.600
and making sure that the architecture is intact

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:28.040
and making sure that the developers

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:29.680
are following the best practices.

00:28:29.680 --> 00:28:32.840
They are participating in core reviews and so on.

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:36.960
Whereas the engineering manager are more towards process,

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:39.480
people, technical excellence,

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:42.480
making sure the strategy is intact.

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:44.520
If they do have any questions

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:48.640
or if they do wanna implement specific things

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:49.480
and give feedback,

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:51.880
they would necessarily work with the tech lead

00:28:51.880 --> 00:28:54.920
and provide that feedback so that they work well together

00:28:54.920 --> 00:28:58.600
and make sure that the technical lead is responsible for it.

00:28:58.600 --> 00:29:01.200
But ultimately the leadership of the team

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:04.400
is more on the engineering manager versus the tech lead

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:07.040
because engineering manager is responsible

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:08.720
for management of people as well.

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:11.800
Engineering management role is more of a leadership role

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:15.000
whereas a tech lead is less of a leadership role.

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.800
They are still considered on the IC path

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:21.320
and may not be included in specific leadership meetings

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.760
because they are necessarily more tied

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:26.320
to the technical aspects of things.

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:29.200
An engineering manager would be going into those meetings,

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:31.560
understanding the strategy, giving feedback,

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:32.920
talking to stakeholders

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:36.680
and then partnering with tech lead to give that information

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:38.440
and share that information with the team.

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:39.600
So there's that barrier,

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:41.040
but sometimes technical leads

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:43.080
have that management experience as well.

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:46.600
So they are also invited to those specific meetings as well.

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:48.920
Tech lead are also more hands-on

00:29:48.920 --> 00:29:50.720
compared to engineering manager

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:52.120
and they are responsible

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:54.640
for the delivery of specific features

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:58.320
whereas engineering managers are less hands-on

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:01.120
and depending on the responsibilities

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:03.360
and the scope of the position,

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:05.400
they may not even code, for example,

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:07.080
because there is a tech lead role.

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:08.720
But in a lot of companies,

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:10.880
there is no tech lead involved at all.

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:12.680
In that case, an engineering manager

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:15.120
does play a tech lead role

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:19.040
or they look for lead developers within the team

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:22.960
that are interested in potentially becoming tech leads

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:25.760
and engineering manager works with them

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:29.120
to become technical leads and help grow them

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:32.560
and improve their technical skills and leadership skills.

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:33.680
Because as a tech lead,

00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:35.520
you do need to have leadership skills

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.440
and that is why it can be confusing

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:40.120
because there is a overlap of skills.

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:41.600
So what does growth look like

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:43.760
after you become an engineering manager?

00:30:43.760 --> 00:30:46.400
Well, you can become a senior engineering manager

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:47.840
and the difference between the two

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:52.000
is engineering manager does lead a team of developers

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:56.440
but a senior engineering manager leads a team of managers.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:59.080
So they have several engineering managers

00:30:59.080 --> 00:31:00.440
reporting under them.

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:02.800
I have been both an engineering manager

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:04.640
and a senior engineering manager

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:07.480
and I was working towards being a director.

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:09.880
So a director is more or less responsible

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:11.960
for a specific domain.

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:15.240
They look at things more holistically

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:18.120
beyond what a senior engineering manager would do.

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:20.120
They're responsible for a really big domain

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:22.080
and making sure the senior engineering managers

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:23.240
have what they need

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:27.200
and they are taking a look at things even more holistically.

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:29.960
So it can happen that being a director,

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:34.320
you are responsible for a even bigger domain

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:36.640
and you have a lot more pressure

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:38.120
and stress because of it.

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:40.520
Similarly, senior director is the same idea.

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:43.240
A few directors are reporting into senior directors

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:45.200
and same thing for VP as well.

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:49.400
A VP overlooks the entirety of engineering

00:31:49.400 --> 00:31:52.000
and they have a bunch of directors

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:54.960
and senior directors reporting into the VP

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:57.760
and the VP is most likely reporting into the CTO

00:31:57.760 --> 00:31:59.080
and so on, right?

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:02.280
But again, like there are so many different positions

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:05.280
in tech, especially on the leadership track

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.360
that you can go on to if you just wanna climb the ladder.

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:10.480
If you don't want to, that's completely okay.

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:12.040
If you decide that, hey,

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:14.400
I just wanna be a engineering manager,

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:15.400
I don't wanna grow more,

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:17.360
I don't wanna take more responsibility,

00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:18.680
that's completely okay

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:21.640
because you can just focus on that specific position.

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:22.880
The more you grow,

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:25.960
the more responsibilities you are going to have

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:27.360
as a developer.

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:30.400
So you really need to assess where you see yourself

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:32.200
in five years or 10 years.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:34.280
You may not know now and that's completely okay.

00:32:34.280 --> 00:32:37.400
You can try a position and figure out if you like it or not

00:32:37.400 --> 00:32:40.000
and that is why it is my recommendation to all developers

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.120
to if there is an opportunity

00:32:42.120 --> 00:32:44.560
of a leadership position within the team

00:32:44.560 --> 00:32:47.560
or if there's an opportunity of getting senior,

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:49.960
even if you are on the IC track,

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:52.320
take that position and first try it out

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:54.720
and see if you like it and if you do like it,

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:55.880
then work with your manager

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:58.640
or potentially a promotion path for you

00:32:58.640 --> 00:33:02.800
because you have already proven yourself for those skills.

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:04.160
So I really hope this makes sense.

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:07.800
There is a website called levels.fyi.

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:09.840
This is a really good website

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:13.240
to check what the different levels are in every company,

00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:16.240
what salaries every position should have

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:18.640
and how it all works together

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:20.640
because this is extremely important for you

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:22.800
before you join a team or a company.

00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:25.080
Look at levels.fyi and take a look

00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:28.720
at the different positions and where you stand as well.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:30.640
I highly recommend you to reach out to someone

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:32.840
in the company and understand

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:34.520
what the different positions are

00:33:34.520 --> 00:33:37.040
so that you know where you stand

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:39.840
and can apply for the position accordingly.

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:41.920
Now, it can be very easy to say

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:44.160
based on all these titles that we looked at

00:33:44.160 --> 00:33:46.480
that TechLead is supposed to do this

00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:49.200
or staff engineer is only supposed to do that

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:51.360
or an engineering manager is only tied

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:54.040
to maybe the people management responsibilities.

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:56.200
But every company is so different

00:33:56.200 --> 00:33:59.800
that you might see that there's a lot of overlap

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:02.560
and that is why it could lead to a lot of confusion

00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:06.000
when you're looking for a job as a leader

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:08.280
or as a developer in general.

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:09.920
So make sure you take a look

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:11.960
at the different positions in the company.

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:15.000
And depending on the scope of the org,

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.040
like how big your company is

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:18.560
and the scope of the work,

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:21.040
there's also a staff developer involved

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:22.920
and potentially a principal developer

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:26.560
that oversees even a bigger domain or a specific domain.

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:30.400
So for example, if let's say you wanna make sure

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:33.760
that the notifications in your company

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:35.760
are properly taken care of,

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:38.400
there should be some sort of notification architecture

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:41.480
that's there for maybe mobile web and so on.

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:43.880
So how will the architecture look like?

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:46.200
And in that case, maybe a technical lead

00:34:46.200 --> 00:34:50.560
maybe tied to a small team will not be able to handle that

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:52.960
and might not have the domain expertise.

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:56.080
And there needs to be someone like a principal developer

00:34:56.080 --> 00:34:59.560
that looks at notifications for the entire company

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:01.080
and works with different teams

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:03.040
implementing those notifications.

00:35:03.040 --> 00:35:04.600
So maybe works with the mobile team

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:06.400
working for mobile notification,

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:09.600
works with the web team for web notifications and so on

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:11.680
so that from a user perspective,

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:13.960
those notifications are the same

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:17.320
and users don't need to know the technical architecture

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:19.560
and all the behind the scenes of the code.

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:21.920
So again, depending on your company,

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:23.880
these roles can be very different

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:26.760
but it's important to know which company you're going for.

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:30.580
So accordingly, you can go for the position as well.

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:32.640
Now, here is my ask for you.

00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:35.520
If you join a company and if you notice that

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:37.880
there are no career ladders

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:40.680
or expectations are not clearly defined,

00:35:40.680 --> 00:35:43.480
please work with your manager,

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:46.360
please work with your HR and give feedback to them

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:49.360
to make sure that these do get implemented

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:50.680
because without these,

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:53.800
it's incredibly difficult for developers to grow

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:56.480
and otherwise one-on-ones with your manager

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:59.120
are just going to be things you just talk about

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:01.880
and there is no action being implemented.

00:36:01.880 --> 00:36:03.760
Or if you do give this feedback

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:06.980
and if your manager says that you need to work on X thing

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:08.360
and if you do work on it,

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:11.960
suddenly there's a Y thing that was never talked about

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:15.560
which probably your manager heard from some other manager

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:18.080
who probably has more influence in the company.

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:21.320
So make sure that everything is documented

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:24.400
so that you know exactly what it needs

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:29.080
and what you need to get promoted within the company.

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:31.160
Now, if there's no career path

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:33.080
or if there is no documentation,

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:35.480
you can take the initiator to define it.

00:36:35.480 --> 00:36:37.720
It doesn't matter what level you are at.

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:39.720
In fact, the more initiative you take

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:41.380
and the more support you get,

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:43.240
this will start to get more defined

00:36:43.240 --> 00:36:45.440
for all developers within the company

00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:48.520
and having a process will really help in this case.

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:52.240
So I really urge you to create a process

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:55.440
or create something in your company if it doesn't exist

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:58.600
and try to get an alignment from HR or your manager

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:01.160
to begin with just to get started.

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:03.640
I really hope this gives you a solid understanding

00:37:03.640 --> 00:37:06.200
for what to look out for when you're looking for a job,

00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:07.840
the different titles in the industry,

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:09.720
the different career paths and so on.

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:11.400
So let's take a look at this mind map

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:14.360
that I have created for us to walk you through exactly

00:37:14.360 --> 00:37:17.240
what day in the life of an engineering manager looks like.

00:37:17.240 --> 00:37:18.800
You don't necessarily need to do

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:20.440
all of these things in a day,

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:23.040
but these are some things that you need to do

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:27.000
and are expected to do in your role as an engineering manager.

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:29.160
So you can think of this mind map as a checklist

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:30.920
of things that you should be doing.

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:32.200
So if you're doing them, great.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:34.440
If you're not, and if you're curious about what it takes,

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:35.720
become an engineering manager,

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:37.320
then this is a great place for you.

00:37:37.320 --> 00:37:38.160
So let's take a look.

00:37:38.160 --> 00:37:40.080
So take a look at this mind map.

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:42.960
There are several parts of this mind map,

00:37:42.960 --> 00:37:44.460
and I don't want you to be scared

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:48.080
because I have basically listed down all the things

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:51.320
that most engineering managers are expected to do.

00:37:51.320 --> 00:37:54.280
Now keep this in mind that every org and team

00:37:54.280 --> 00:37:55.120
are quite different.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:58.680
So you might be expected to do more things or less things

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:01.400
if they already have a role for that,

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:03.800
if they already have a position for it.

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:08.080
Because engineering management and project management

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:12.080
and a lot of it, it has a ton of overlap.

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:13.280
If there are roles for that,

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:15.800
you might not necessarily have to do those things,

00:38:15.800 --> 00:38:17.800
but if not, then you might have to.

00:38:17.800 --> 00:38:21.520
So I'm gonna cover a pretty robust roadmap,

00:38:21.520 --> 00:38:24.200
but keep this in mind so that you don't get scared

00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:26.300
that you might have to do everything in this.

00:38:26.300 --> 00:38:28.240
This specific engineering manager roadmap

00:38:28.240 --> 00:38:31.080
is divided into a few different pillars.

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:35.600
One is organization, team, people, hiring, product,

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:38.560
technical, communication, and personal growth.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:41.120
So let's take a look at each one by one.

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:43.120
If you like a copy of this mind map,

00:38:43.120 --> 00:38:44.480
you can download it for free.

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:46.200
Take a look at the description below if you like,

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:48.180
and you can have a copy of your own.

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:50.280
So the first thing that we are going to take a look at

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:51.560
is organization.

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:55.280
I would like you to have a top-down view of your org

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:59.400
so that you are able to know exactly how your role

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:02.840
as an engineering manager plays in the wider org

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:06.580
and how goals are set, how you create objectives

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:07.680
for your teams, and so on.

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:09.440
Let's start to zoom in on the org.

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:11.360
So there are a few things you need to keep in mind

00:39:11.360 --> 00:39:14.120
as we discuss about the org structure.

00:39:14.120 --> 00:39:16.920
So the first is you need to have a really solid understanding

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:18.720
of the vision of your org

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:20.880
and what the early vision looks like.

00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:23.720
So every year, maybe towards the end of the year

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:25.400
for the upcoming year,

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:28.140
the founders or the senior leaders meet

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:30.840
and they discuss what the vision for the org

00:39:30.840 --> 00:39:33.800
should look like in the next year or the next five years

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:34.800
and so on.

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:36.760
And accordingly, they decide they want to reach

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:40.120
to X milestone or X goals and X number,

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:42.080
and that's how they decide, okay,

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:44.320
the early vision of this specific year

00:39:44.320 --> 00:39:47.040
or the upcoming year is going to look whatever.

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:50.440
Now you need to understand that vision very clearly

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:52.600
because then you are going to make decisions

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:54.360
for your team based on that,

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:56.400
and you will understand how your team plays a role

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:57.280
in that vision.

00:39:57.280 --> 00:40:00.600
If you create goals for your teams against those goals,

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:05.360
then I kind of think of your team as a tiny boat

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:06.400
which is steering away,

00:40:06.400 --> 00:40:10.520
which is going to slowly turn the ship away

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:12.200
because all these tiny teams

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:14.440
or all these tiny boats for the ship

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:17.040
are not heading in the right direction.

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:20.480
So your ship is going to slowly start turning away

00:40:20.480 --> 00:40:22.600
and that's not what we want.

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:25.400
So have a good understanding of the org.

00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:28.520
After you understand what the vision of your org looks like,

00:40:28.520 --> 00:40:30.560
try to understand the business vision

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:32.360
and the technical vision as well.

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:36.520
So based on the business vision, the VP, the CTO,

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:38.400
or whoever that might be,

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:40.280
your senior leaders, senior technical leaders

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:43.400
are also going to create a vision for your org.

00:40:43.400 --> 00:40:46.520
So for example, we should move away from REST APIs

00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:48.160
and only use GraphQL,

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:52.200
or we should make sure that all our experiences

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:55.680
are fully accessible and accessibility is a priority

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:58.680
for every user experience that we create, which it should be.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:01.840
So whatever that might be, we are going to,

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:03.200
you need to know that vision

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:05.720
so that you have a better understanding for your team.

00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:08.320
So for example, if accessibility is part of it,

00:41:08.320 --> 00:41:10.760
then you need to make sure that when you are creating

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:12.440
your roadmap for your team,

00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:14.960
accessibility is added as the criteria

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:17.120
because that's also part of the vision.

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:18.560
The next thing is planning.

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:21.960
Now, based on that vision and the goals of your business,

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:24.280
you need to understand which goals apply to you

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:26.680
because there might be goals for the entire company.

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:29.160
Based on those goals, then you need to start planning

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:31.840
and planning your yearly roadmap

00:41:31.840 --> 00:41:34.160
and then your quarterly roadmap as well.

00:41:34.160 --> 00:41:36.200
So based on that, you're going to assess.

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:40.000
You wanna achieve X percent in a year.

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:41.440
This is our goal.

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:44.680
And then based on that, for every quarter,

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:47.680
we are going to try to meet X number, right?

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:50.000
Or we are going to try to hit $10 million

00:41:50.000 --> 00:41:51.120
or whatever that might be.

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:54.120
Although, how many dollars does it associate to?

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:56.080
It's not necessarily in your control.

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:58.480
You are also partnering with product and design

00:41:58.480 --> 00:42:02.280
and all the other departments as well, marketing and so on.

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:06.440
But you need to assess what the quarterly goals look like.

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:09.160
And then accordingly set ambitious goals.

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:14.080
Now, one framework that you can use to divide those big goals

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:16.120
into smaller goals is OKRs.

00:42:16.120 --> 00:42:18.960
OKRs stand for objective key results.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:21.520
And this is a framework where you can assess

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:23.920
how you can set a ambitious objective

00:42:23.920 --> 00:42:25.560
and associate that to a key result

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:28.240
you wanna associate yourself with.

00:42:29.640 --> 00:42:32.680
So I won't go too deep into what OKRs are,

00:42:32.680 --> 00:42:34.440
but this should give you a good understanding

00:42:34.440 --> 00:42:37.400
that it's the framework that is quite commonly used

00:42:37.400 --> 00:42:39.480
while planning for the year

00:42:39.480 --> 00:42:41.480
and while planning the roadmap.

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:44.640
The next thing is collaboration with HR partner.

00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:47.680
So for your org, whenever you have to,

00:42:47.680 --> 00:42:49.560
you also need to understand the decisions

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:50.720
that the org needs to make.

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:51.800
There's gonna be an HR.

00:42:51.800 --> 00:42:53.080
In every HR department,

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:56.160
you are going to have a partner that you collaborate with.

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:58.400
And that partner is going to help you communicate

00:42:58.400 --> 00:43:02.040
exactly what they need you to communicate to your team.

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:05.200
So make sure that you have a really strong relationship

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:06.480
with the HR partner

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:08.560
and if there is any downturn communication

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:10.800
or if there is any important org communication

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:12.120
or anything like that,

00:43:12.120 --> 00:43:15.480
you will be able to know exactly the words to use

00:43:15.480 --> 00:43:17.200
to communicate that to your teams

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:21.240
so that you make sure your team is in the best part possible

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:24.960
and they have all the information necessary as well.

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:26.240
So again, in organization,

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:27.960
these are a few things you need to keep in mind.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:30.840
The next thing we are going to take a look at is team.

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:32.960
Now, team is very important for you

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:34.440
as an engineering manager.

00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:37.000
There are quite a few things you need to do for your team

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:41.000
just to make sure that your team is in the right spot

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:44.240
and you have full control as a leader.

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:47.760
Now, make sure that you have good processes

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:50.240
and there is good documentation of processes.

00:43:50.240 --> 00:43:52.680
You're iterating on those processes as well.

00:43:52.680 --> 00:43:54.560
Again, too much process is not good.

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:57.880
Have a right balance for process and task management

00:43:57.880 --> 00:44:01.040
such as who gets to do, who gets to work on what.

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:02.920
You need to strike a really good balance here

00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:05.440
because you don't want to get assigned tasks to folks

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:07.920
that are not necessarily interested in those tasks.

00:44:07.920 --> 00:44:09.920
So make sure you have a good understanding

00:44:09.920 --> 00:44:12.440
of the people working on that task

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:16.200
and also they are motivated to work on those tasks as well

00:44:16.200 --> 00:44:18.040
and give them full autonomy as well.

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:20.760
So for example, some people do create for autonomy.

00:44:20.760 --> 00:44:23.000
So for example, if they do love autonomy

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:27.080
then assign them a task, talk to them about a high level goals

00:44:27.080 --> 00:44:29.000
and let them have full control

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:31.000
in terms of how they want to execute it.

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:33.440
And you can be the sounding partner for them

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:36.840
and understand how they plan to implement it and so on.

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:39.360
But based on what the interests are,

00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:41.440
what the strengths and weaknesses are,

00:44:41.440 --> 00:44:43.320
accordingly assign tasks as well.

00:44:43.320 --> 00:44:46.440
Then you need to also measure your team performance.

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:49.360
So for team performance, anything you want to measure,

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:52.000
it needs to be tracked so that you are able to measure.

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:56.480
So assign proper metrics and track them,

00:44:56.480 --> 00:44:58.920
conduct weekly retrospectives.

00:44:58.920 --> 00:45:02.040
Retrospectives are a way to reflect on how the week went,

00:45:02.040 --> 00:45:04.920
what went well, what didn't go so well and so on.

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:07.200
But this is how you can measure the team performance.

00:45:07.200 --> 00:45:11.400
For example, if your team's weekly output

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:13.840
is a certain number of tasks,

00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:17.560
then make sure that you are able to maintain that momentum.

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:19.520
If you are not, then your project planning

00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:21.440
might not go as planned.

00:45:21.440 --> 00:45:23.800
So make sure that you have a good understanding

00:45:23.800 --> 00:45:25.080
of all of that.

00:45:25.080 --> 00:45:26.840
The next thing is project management.

00:45:26.840 --> 00:45:30.200
Now this is something that you need to understand

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:32.400
that there might be another role altogether

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:33.600
in every company.

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:35.000
Now every company is so different.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:36.800
So if you don't have this,

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:39.120
then you might have to do it on your own

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:41.800
or you have to gather around the developers

00:45:41.800 --> 00:45:45.080
and assign these roles and take turns doing them.

00:45:45.080 --> 00:45:46.520
So for project management,

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:48.320
you need to plan the milestones.

00:45:48.320 --> 00:45:50.960
So for example, what are some tasks and milestones

00:45:50.960 --> 00:45:52.680
you need to hit along the way?

00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:55.680
Because the project can be maybe four to six months long

00:45:55.680 --> 00:45:56.840
or one to two months long,

00:45:56.840 --> 00:45:59.720
but what are the many milestones that you can hit on the way

00:45:59.720 --> 00:46:02.040
just to showcase to your stakeholders

00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:04.440
and to your team and product managers

00:46:04.440 --> 00:46:07.240
and everyone that you are heading in the right path?

00:46:08.200 --> 00:46:10.600
Then you need to monitor that performance.

00:46:10.600 --> 00:46:11.840
You need to make sure that you have

00:46:11.840 --> 00:46:13.280
good productivity practices.

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:16.400
So if you do know that there are certain things

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:19.480
that are maybe creating a lot more toil for your team,

00:46:19.480 --> 00:46:23.520
then maybe invest some time into automating that

00:46:23.520 --> 00:46:26.400
just because you don't want your team to be wasting time

00:46:26.400 --> 00:46:28.760
on things that where they can be productive.

00:46:28.760 --> 00:46:31.520
So make sure that you do invest time there.

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:34.480
Then is prioritization, grooming, planning.

00:46:34.480 --> 00:46:37.800
So plan your tickets properly, plan your tasks properly,

00:46:37.800 --> 00:46:42.520
make sure that the backlog of tickets is properly groomed

00:46:42.520 --> 00:46:46.000
and make sure that you understand what the priorities are

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:48.400
based on the roadmap that you have planned as well.

00:46:48.400 --> 00:46:50.880
But again, if the tick tasks have proper details,

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:53.360
then the developers will be able to work on them as well,

00:46:53.360 --> 00:46:55.000
but obviously gather around the developers

00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:57.480
and make sure you work on all of these.

00:46:57.480 --> 00:46:59.360
Then is continuous improvement.

00:46:59.360 --> 00:47:01.840
Any of those will keep changing every month.

00:47:01.840 --> 00:47:03.840
Now, this is just a good list to have,

00:47:03.840 --> 00:47:05.920
but you need to make sure that you keep improving,

00:47:05.920 --> 00:47:07.400
you keep iterating with your team.

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:09.760
Now, for every team, different things are going to work.

00:47:09.760 --> 00:47:12.480
Not everything is going to work for every single team.

00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:14.000
So make sure you cater to that

00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:17.240
so one team can differ from the other and so on.

00:47:17.240 --> 00:47:20.880
A very important pillar of engineering management

00:47:20.880 --> 00:47:22.000
are the people.

00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:24.000
So this is something you need to handle

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:25.560
in a very delicate way

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:29.000
because your decisions have a huge impact on your team

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:30.920
and people are going to be impacted.

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:33.960
So make sure that you know exactly what you're doing.

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:36.160
Always create a plan for it.

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:39.080
Make sure that the decision-makers with you

00:47:39.080 --> 00:47:41.440
are also on the same page

00:47:41.440 --> 00:47:45.120
when you do take a look at anything to deal with people.

00:47:45.120 --> 00:47:46.800
So because you really want to make sure you get this right.

00:47:46.800 --> 00:47:50.920
So in people, you have to hire and onboard the people

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:52.360
that you work with.

00:47:52.360 --> 00:47:55.960
Now, if you have a team of maybe two developers,

00:47:55.960 --> 00:47:57.480
if you want to make a team,

00:47:57.480 --> 00:48:00.120
if you want to create a team of seven developers,

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:03.960
and five to seven is usually the max for a team size

00:48:03.960 --> 00:48:08.160
or a healthy max for a team size for one engineering manager.

00:48:08.160 --> 00:48:09.800
And in that case, you need to make sure

00:48:09.800 --> 00:48:11.680
that you're hiring the right people

00:48:11.680 --> 00:48:13.200
and onboarding them as well.

00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:15.480
And hiring and onboarding is a separate pillar

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:17.680
for this reason because there's lots to talk about it.

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:21.000
So we will take a look at it very soon.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:23.520
Next is the happiness of the people.

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:27.120
It's very important that you foster a culture

00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:31.240
of transparency, you create a psychological safety for them.

00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:33.680
And I do have a blog post written on this topic.

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:36.720
If you're interested, take a look at the description,

00:48:36.720 --> 00:48:39.480
but make sure that these things do align

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:43.680
with the team culture and people are happier in general

00:48:43.680 --> 00:48:46.480
because this will add to the culture of your team.

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:48.760
If people are happier, people are excited to wake up

00:48:48.760 --> 00:48:50.600
in the morning and come work for you,

00:48:50.600 --> 00:48:53.960
then you are going to be more successful as a happy outcome.

00:48:53.960 --> 00:48:56.400
People are going to be more productive because of that

00:48:56.400 --> 00:48:59.600
because they are interested in making the company better.

00:48:59.600 --> 00:49:02.760
So make sure that the happiness is really accounted for.

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:04.040
The next thing is feedback.

00:49:04.040 --> 00:49:05.400
The way you gather feedback

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:08.320
and the way you give constructive feedback

00:49:08.320 --> 00:49:09.920
is extremely important.

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:12.760
Now, feedback is so important that you really need

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:14.720
to make sure that you build really good frameworks

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:16.640
and patterns for people

00:49:16.640 --> 00:49:19.160
because different people accept feedback differently.

00:49:19.160 --> 00:49:24.160
Different people ask for feedback in a very different way.

00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:27.080
So make sure that you gather feedback about them

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:28.320
in a proper way,

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:31.760
and you also give them constructive feedback as well.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:33.200
Then is career growth.

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:35.400
Now, career growth is extremely important

00:49:35.400 --> 00:49:36.760
in a developer's life.

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:39.400
The developers reporting into you really are interested

00:49:39.400 --> 00:49:41.000
to know where they stand,

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:43.120
what can they do to get to the next level,

00:49:43.120 --> 00:49:46.960
and there should be full transparency related to this.

00:49:46.960 --> 00:49:50.200
So first, are there any career ladders set up

00:49:50.200 --> 00:49:51.040
in the company?

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:52.960
And career ladders could be things like

00:49:52.960 --> 00:49:55.080
if they are a intermediate developer,

00:49:55.080 --> 00:49:57.320
then maybe they want to get to senior developer

00:49:57.320 --> 00:50:01.880
or staff developer, principal developer, and so on.

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:04.000
Now, depending on the level you are at,

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:06.800
there could be different levels in every company.

00:50:06.800 --> 00:50:09.160
So the career ladder can be completely different

00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:10.440
in every company.

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:14.040
Now, if they want to take the individual contributor path,

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:15.120
then they can,

00:50:15.120 --> 00:50:19.080
or if they want to take maybe a staff technical leader out,

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:20.560
then they can do that as well.

00:50:20.560 --> 00:50:24.320
Now again, depending on the team that you work on,

00:50:24.320 --> 00:50:26.040
or a staff or a technical lead

00:50:26.040 --> 00:50:28.160
can be considered a leadership position,

00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:30.240
or it could be considered an IC position,

00:50:30.240 --> 00:50:32.040
an engineering manager onwards

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:34.920
is considered a management position or a leadership position.

00:50:34.920 --> 00:50:38.400
So again, career ladders differ from company to company,

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:42.240
but make sure that you know what path they want to go on

00:50:42.240 --> 00:50:46.080
so that you can give them opportunities accordingly.

00:50:46.080 --> 00:50:50.600
Make sure that every developer is creating smart goals.

00:50:50.600 --> 00:50:53.480
Smart is a framework used to create goals,

00:50:53.480 --> 00:50:57.640
and I will link what smart means in the description below,

00:50:57.640 --> 00:51:01.120
but they allow you to create really smart goals.

00:51:01.120 --> 00:51:03.440
Then make sure that they do have a development plan

00:51:03.440 --> 00:51:04.520
or a growth plan.

00:51:04.520 --> 00:51:06.920
Again, different companies call it different things,

00:51:06.920 --> 00:51:09.800
but a growth plan allows you to document

00:51:09.800 --> 00:51:11.720
where they're at in their journey,

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:14.160
how their goals can tie to the objectives

00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:15.400
that they're working on,

00:51:15.400 --> 00:51:18.120
and those objectives align to the company's vision.

00:51:18.120 --> 00:51:21.760
And this is why I kind of gave you an idea of the org vision,

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:23.320
the network map early vision,

00:51:23.320 --> 00:51:25.800
and then these goals should map to that

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:27.440
so all the developers are working

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:29.240
to make the company better.

00:51:29.240 --> 00:51:30.800
Then is the BRAG document.

00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:33.600
A BRAG document is a document

00:51:33.600 --> 00:51:37.400
where you document exactly all the cool things

00:51:37.400 --> 00:51:39.800
that you have done and things that you are proud of.

00:51:39.800 --> 00:51:41.880
So make sure every developer in your team

00:51:41.880 --> 00:51:45.120
has a specific BRAG doc that they are proud of.

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:48.480
And make sure there are also promotions planned out

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:50.120
and mapped out as well.

00:51:50.120 --> 00:51:53.280
So if they are already doing a good job

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:54.800
and meeting expectations

00:51:54.800 --> 00:51:58.720
or even exceeding expectations in a specific level,

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:02.120
don't wait for them to ask you to promote them.

00:52:02.120 --> 00:52:03.640
When you think they are ready,

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:05.920
start creating a plan with them

00:52:05.920 --> 00:52:08.920
just to make sure that their career growth is on track.

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:11.520
So again, all of these things are very important.

00:52:11.520 --> 00:52:12.800
Then is performance reviews.

00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:15.280
The performance reviews are extremely important.

00:52:15.280 --> 00:52:17.080
Now, one thing you want to really avoid,

00:52:17.080 --> 00:52:18.880
a one mistake that you really want to avoid

00:52:18.880 --> 00:52:21.360
is wait until the very last minute

00:52:21.360 --> 00:52:24.160
to give a performance review to the person

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:27.760
or to give a feedback to the specific developer.

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:29.920
Anything that comes out of performance reviews,

00:52:29.920 --> 00:52:32.800
none of it should come as a surprise to the developer.

00:52:32.800 --> 00:52:35.560
If they're surprised, you have not done a good job

00:52:35.560 --> 00:52:37.200
as an engineering manager.

00:52:37.200 --> 00:52:39.600
So make sure that your performance reviews

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:43.920
are really on point and you already have a document

00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:46.760
which is your growth plan that ties it all together.

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:49.240
Hiring the right people in your team

00:52:49.240 --> 00:52:52.440
is going to be majority of the battle for you.

00:52:52.440 --> 00:52:55.560
If you have really good people working for you,

00:52:55.560 --> 00:52:58.400
then it's going to be easier for you to meet your goals,

00:52:58.400 --> 00:53:00.880
your org goals, and as a result,

00:53:00.880 --> 00:53:02.480
it's going to be really good

00:53:02.480 --> 00:53:04.340
for your career growth as well.

00:53:04.340 --> 00:53:06.080
So make sure that you hire right.

00:53:06.080 --> 00:53:08.200
If you don't hire the right people,

00:53:08.200 --> 00:53:10.120
it can have a serious impact

00:53:10.120 --> 00:53:11.920
and that is why you need to make sure

00:53:11.920 --> 00:53:14.600
you have good practices set up and as well.

00:53:14.600 --> 00:53:18.400
So let's talk about what happens once you hire.

00:53:18.400 --> 00:53:19.440
So once you do hire,

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:22.120
make sure you have really good onboarding set up for them

00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:24.100
because your goal as an engineering manager

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:26.720
should be that they don't take the time to onboard,

00:53:26.720 --> 00:53:29.040
but it doesn't take too long for them to onboard

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:33.120
because you have set up the right processes in place

00:53:33.120 --> 00:53:34.940
and everything that they need to do

00:53:34.940 --> 00:53:38.920
and need to know to get up and running quickly in their job.

00:53:38.920 --> 00:53:41.040
So make sure there's proper documentation.

00:53:41.040 --> 00:53:43.920
You have set up 30, 60, 90 day goals for them.

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:47.240
So they have some sort of a metric to head

00:53:47.240 --> 00:53:51.000
so that they can work on those goals in their 30 days,

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:52.920
in their 60 days, and in their 90 days.

00:53:52.920 --> 00:53:56.440
So make sure their first 90 days are set up for success.

00:53:56.440 --> 00:53:59.520
I have written a ebook on your first 90 days

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:01.040
as an engineering leader

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:02.440
and everything that you need to do

00:54:02.440 --> 00:54:05.360
to make sure your first 90 days are set up for success.

00:54:05.360 --> 00:54:06.320
If you're interested,

00:54:06.320 --> 00:54:07.880
take a look at the description below.

00:54:07.880 --> 00:54:09.760
The next thing you need to make sure you do

00:54:09.760 --> 00:54:13.420
is go through diversity and unconscious bias training.

00:54:13.420 --> 00:54:15.880
As humans, we all have our own biases

00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:18.340
and we need to make sure when we are hiring

00:54:18.340 --> 00:54:20.160
that those biases don't interfere

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:22.260
with the way we hire people.

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:24.580
That's why it's important that you go through this training

00:54:24.580 --> 00:54:26.280
before you start interviewing people

00:54:26.280 --> 00:54:28.000
because then you're going to know

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:30.240
what are some of the biases that you may have

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:33.400
and how to make sure that those don't play a role

00:54:33.400 --> 00:54:34.640
when you're hiring folks.

00:54:34.640 --> 00:54:36.380
Make sure you partner with recruiting

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:37.760
as you're hiring as well.

00:54:37.760 --> 00:54:41.200
Honestly, recruiters are your best friends during hiring.

00:54:41.200 --> 00:54:43.080
If you tell them what you're looking for,

00:54:43.080 --> 00:54:46.200
they are going to go and find people on LinkedIn

00:54:46.200 --> 00:54:49.840
to make sure that the people are the right fit

00:54:49.840 --> 00:54:51.040
for your specific team.

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:52.080
Then is interviews.

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:53.400
So when you're hiring,

00:54:53.400 --> 00:54:55.960
make sure you have really good processes set up

00:54:55.960 --> 00:54:58.340
to hire folks.

00:54:58.340 --> 00:55:01.520
So make sure there's diverse interview participation.

00:55:01.520 --> 00:55:04.480
There are people from your team interviewing them as well.

00:55:04.480 --> 00:55:07.060
So they are comfortable for the new hires

00:55:07.060 --> 00:55:08.500
that are going to join the team.

00:55:08.500 --> 00:55:11.280
Make sure that there's a good technical challenge set up

00:55:11.280 --> 00:55:13.960
and not something that anyone can just Google.

00:55:13.960 --> 00:55:17.880
Then there's also a section for soft-scale assessment.

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:19.520
Their communication is on point

00:55:19.520 --> 00:55:22.520
so that you know that they are a good fit for your team.

00:55:22.520 --> 00:55:23.920
Conduct resume reviews

00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:26.720
and you also hire for other teams as well.

00:55:26.720 --> 00:55:29.880
So as much as it's important that you hire for your team,

00:55:29.880 --> 00:55:31.160
you will also be expected

00:55:31.160 --> 00:55:33.680
to interview other team members as well.

00:55:33.680 --> 00:55:36.920
So make sure that you're really a important part of it.

00:55:36.920 --> 00:55:38.080
You're hiring for other teams,

00:55:38.080 --> 00:55:40.440
but you're also reviewing resumes

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:43.200
and making sure that you're improving

00:55:43.200 --> 00:55:45.200
the culture of the company.

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:47.920
Hiring is something that you do need

00:55:47.920 --> 00:55:49.960
to invest really good time in

00:55:49.960 --> 00:55:51.460
because as an engineering manager,

00:55:51.460 --> 00:55:54.360
you are going to hire folks for you,

00:55:54.360 --> 00:55:57.040
not just for your team, but also for other teams.

00:55:57.040 --> 00:55:58.720
So understanding their goals

00:55:58.720 --> 00:56:00.920
and hiring the right people will really help you.

00:56:00.920 --> 00:56:04.040
Having a diverse set of interviewers really make

00:56:04.040 --> 00:56:09.040
for a good interview experience for the interview as well.

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:11.960
But again, hiring is going to be a really important part

00:56:11.960 --> 00:56:14.960
of your role and something you should not take it lightly.

00:56:14.960 --> 00:56:19.240
So far, we have talked a lot about different pillars

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:20.880
of engineering management,

00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:23.720
but product is a pillar that is extremely important

00:56:23.720 --> 00:56:25.760
as a engineering manager.

00:56:25.760 --> 00:56:28.920
When you are making decisions as an engineering manager,

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:31.200
you will also have a product counterpart,

00:56:31.200 --> 00:56:34.800
a design counterpart, and maybe several others too,

00:56:34.800 --> 00:56:37.400
just to make sure that all the decisions are being made

00:56:37.400 --> 00:56:39.680
and all the key players are in the room,

00:56:39.680 --> 00:56:42.180
all those decisions are being made for your team.

00:56:42.180 --> 00:56:45.120
Even though the people in your team are reporting to you,

00:56:45.120 --> 00:56:47.120
there might be designers that are reporting

00:56:47.120 --> 00:56:50.640
to a design manager or there might be a product person

00:56:50.640 --> 00:56:54.840
that is reporting to a senior product manager and so on.

00:56:54.840 --> 00:56:58.620
So you need to make sure that you are not the only one

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:01.860
making decisions and having a really tight collaboration

00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:02.700
with product.

00:57:02.700 --> 00:57:05.880
And I should also add to your design is extremely important

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:09.920
or any other folks that are important in decision-making.

00:57:09.920 --> 00:57:11.920
So in product, let's make sure

00:57:11.920 --> 00:57:13.640
that there is business alignment.

00:57:13.640 --> 00:57:16.960
So you understand what the business is looking for.

00:57:16.960 --> 00:57:20.040
And when product does create a strategy

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:23.240
as an engineering manager, you review that strategy

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:26.160
and really are on the same page as well.

00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:28.460
You make sure you ideate on different ideas

00:57:28.460 --> 00:57:30.800
that are really good for the product,

00:57:30.800 --> 00:57:32.280
create a different strategy

00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:35.500
and collaborate on that strategy as well.

00:57:35.500 --> 00:57:37.880
And based on the product strategy,

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:40.880
make sure you also have your technical roadmap representation

00:57:40.880 --> 00:57:42.080
and focus as well.

00:57:42.080 --> 00:57:44.940
So if there's any technical that needs to be added

00:57:44.940 --> 00:57:47.400
or if there's any toll that's being created

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:51.160
because of a specific task on your team,

00:57:51.160 --> 00:57:53.160
then make sure that gets added and represented

00:57:53.160 --> 00:57:54.880
in the technical roadmap as well.

00:57:54.880 --> 00:57:56.880
A technical roadmap is always a good idea

00:57:56.880 --> 00:57:58.960
of features that you would like to improve,

00:57:58.960 --> 00:58:02.500
things that architecture that needs to happen

00:58:02.500 --> 00:58:05.480
to make sure that we sustain the scale

00:58:05.480 --> 00:58:07.160
of the product requirements.

00:58:07.160 --> 00:58:08.800
So again, having a technical roadmap

00:58:08.800 --> 00:58:09.840
would be really important

00:58:09.840 --> 00:58:11.600
and something that you can initiate

00:58:11.600 --> 00:58:13.800
and get the team involved to start creating.

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:16.640
Next is decision-making and critical thinking.

00:58:16.640 --> 00:58:18.860
Always, as I mentioned earlier,

00:58:18.860 --> 00:58:22.600
make sure that all the key decision-makers are in the team

00:58:22.600 --> 00:58:25.920
and never say no, make sure that you use critical thinking

00:58:25.920 --> 00:58:28.560
just to make sure that you don't just say no,

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:31.280
but also are able to come up with creative solutions

00:58:31.280 --> 00:58:34.400
for the problems because again, you need that creativity

00:58:34.400 --> 00:58:36.420
to push the art forward.

00:58:36.420 --> 00:58:39.960
Communicating understanding of technical requirements.

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:43.760
Since you are a technical person in the room,

00:58:43.760 --> 00:58:46.960
you would have to a lot of times communicate the product

00:58:46.960 --> 00:58:48.480
and do other counterparts,

00:58:48.480 --> 00:58:51.760
why certain tasks are taking this long,

00:58:51.760 --> 00:58:53.660
why something needs to happen,

00:58:53.660 --> 00:58:56.080
why the architecture needs to be prioritized

00:58:56.080 --> 00:58:57.440
or whatever that might be,

00:58:57.440 --> 00:58:59.240
make sure that you're communicating that

00:58:59.240 --> 00:59:01.760
and all of you are on the same page.

00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:04.440
And lastly, prioritizing toil and technical debt

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:06.600
in collaboration with product as well.

00:59:06.600 --> 00:59:08.800
So again, if there's any toll that's been created

00:59:08.800 --> 00:59:12.960
because of past products or past features

00:59:12.960 --> 00:59:14.480
that you have implemented

00:59:14.480 --> 00:59:17.800
or anything that just needs to be prioritized,

00:59:17.800 --> 00:59:20.940
then you need to prioritize in the roadmap as well.

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:23.760
But again, making sure everyone is on the same page

00:59:23.760 --> 00:59:26.280
because you're not the only decision-maker in the room.

00:59:26.280 --> 00:59:28.080
So again, collaboration with product

00:59:28.080 --> 00:59:31.680
is extremely important as a engineering manager.

00:59:31.680 --> 00:59:33.920
We should call this technical excellence

00:59:33.920 --> 00:59:35.280
because as an engineering manager,

00:59:35.280 --> 00:59:37.320
you need to make sure that your team

00:59:37.320 --> 00:59:40.120
is following the best practices

00:59:40.120 --> 00:59:42.120
and everything is under control

00:59:42.120 --> 00:59:45.000
from an architecture perspective and so on as well.

00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:46.560
Now again, you don't need to solve

00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:49.240
all of these problems yourself,

00:59:49.240 --> 00:59:52.120
but get the team together to make sure

00:59:52.120 --> 00:59:54.800
that these problems are being solved.

00:59:54.800 --> 00:59:58.760
So for example, in the technical aspect,

00:59:58.760 --> 01:00:01.080
there would be managing on-call

01:00:01.080 --> 01:00:04.120
and if there's any support required

01:00:04.120 --> 01:00:06.400
and there are off-hours of what required,

01:00:06.400 --> 01:00:08.540
then are there best practices for it?

01:00:08.540 --> 01:00:09.960
That you are monitoring toils,

01:00:09.960 --> 01:00:12.040
so you have proper dashboard set up

01:00:12.040 --> 01:00:14.660
just to make sure everything is being tracked.

01:00:14.660 --> 01:00:16.380
If there are any incidents

01:00:16.380 --> 01:00:18.600
that have created toll in the past,

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:20.040
an incident could be something,

01:00:20.040 --> 01:00:21.360
let's say the system goes down

01:00:21.360 --> 01:00:23.400
because of a feature that you own

01:00:23.400 --> 01:00:26.200
or if there was a massive impact to the customers

01:00:26.200 --> 01:00:28.080
and make sure that you have

01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:30.560
incident management practices as well.

01:00:30.560 --> 01:00:31.720
There's service ownership,

01:00:31.720 --> 01:00:34.000
so anything that you create, you own.

01:00:34.000 --> 01:00:36.880
So any of the services that you own

01:00:36.880 --> 01:00:39.600
truly belongs to you and your team

01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:41.240
and you truly own that.

01:00:41.240 --> 01:00:43.960
If you have any architectural best practices

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:47.360
documented as well so that anyone new

01:00:47.360 --> 01:00:50.800
that any new team collaborating with your team

01:00:50.800 --> 01:00:52.040
can refer that document

01:00:52.040 --> 01:00:54.880
just to make sure everything is on point.

01:00:54.880 --> 01:00:57.520
Then there is documentation and technical analysis

01:00:57.520 --> 01:01:01.040
being done as well for every feature that you work on

01:01:01.040 --> 01:01:04.280
so that anytime any new feature needs to be worked on,

01:01:04.280 --> 01:01:05.640
there's documentation

01:01:05.640 --> 01:01:07.480
and there's a source of truth for everything,

01:01:07.480 --> 01:01:10.200
so just make sure that this is being done

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:11.580
and again, you don't need to do it,

01:01:11.580 --> 01:01:13.120
you can get your team,

01:01:13.120 --> 01:01:16.700
you can delegate these tasks to your team to do it as well.

01:01:16.700 --> 01:01:18.520
Then there is technical roadmap

01:01:18.520 --> 01:01:20.040
and alignment and technical strategy

01:01:20.040 --> 01:01:22.480
which aligns with what we talked about in product

01:01:22.480 --> 01:01:25.120
and then there are good code quality processes

01:01:25.120 --> 01:01:27.720
and templates created for your team

01:01:27.720 --> 01:01:29.720
which allows your team to write better code.

01:01:29.720 --> 01:01:32.040
So for example, there are better code review practices,

01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:33.840
there's a no blame culture,

01:01:33.840 --> 01:01:37.000
you are using pair programming as a way to collaborate

01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:39.680
and get developers together and improve their skills

01:01:39.680 --> 01:01:41.040
and so on and lastly,

01:01:41.040 --> 01:01:44.360
you have proper practices for automatic deploys

01:01:44.360 --> 01:01:47.760
which allows you to automate your entire process in general.

01:01:47.760 --> 01:01:49.560
So anything that can be automated,

01:01:49.560 --> 01:01:52.120
you can think of technical bucket or technical pillar

01:01:52.120 --> 01:01:54.240
as a technical excellence bucket

01:01:54.240 --> 01:01:55.600
which allows you to make sure

01:01:55.600 --> 01:01:59.280
that the technical excellence for your team is really great

01:01:59.280 --> 01:02:00.880
and make sure that you are following

01:02:00.880 --> 01:02:03.520
all the best practices possible while deploying

01:02:03.520 --> 01:02:05.880
because otherwise you're just going to create more toil

01:02:05.880 --> 01:02:08.320
which will impact customers eventually.

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:10.080
So again, technical is really important.

01:02:10.080 --> 01:02:12.320
Communication is a very important pillar

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:14.200
as a engineering manager.

01:02:14.200 --> 01:02:16.680
You need to make sure that you're communicating right

01:02:16.680 --> 01:02:20.720
and effectively, frequently with anyone

01:02:20.720 --> 01:02:22.080
who you're working with

01:02:22.080 --> 01:02:24.520
so that you all are on the same page

01:02:24.520 --> 01:02:27.440
and there are no misunderstandings in your work culture.

01:02:27.440 --> 01:02:30.200
So for example, if you're working cross-team,

01:02:30.200 --> 01:02:32.080
then there is proper collaboration

01:02:32.080 --> 01:02:34.280
and practices with cross-teams

01:02:34.280 --> 01:02:37.280
that people are talking to each other,

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:39.160
there's proper meeting facilitation,

01:02:39.160 --> 01:02:40.960
the teams are working well together.

01:02:40.960 --> 01:02:43.920
Make sure that you partner with the engineering manager

01:02:43.920 --> 01:02:46.960
or the product manager or the design manager

01:02:46.960 --> 01:02:51.600
or whoever the leaders are of that team

01:02:51.600 --> 01:02:53.560
so that you all are on the same page

01:02:53.560 --> 01:02:55.600
and collaboration is seamless.

01:02:55.600 --> 01:02:56.520
Towards the end of the day,

01:02:56.520 --> 01:02:58.000
all the team members should be happy

01:02:58.000 --> 01:02:59.520
that the collaboration happened

01:02:59.520 --> 01:03:02.520
so that everyone is happier.

01:03:02.520 --> 01:03:06.440
Then if there are proper async communication practices,

01:03:06.440 --> 01:03:09.160
after 2020, we are in a spot

01:03:09.160 --> 01:03:11.800
where a lot of us are in a remote culture.

01:03:11.800 --> 01:03:15.600
So it's important that communication practices are async

01:03:15.600 --> 01:03:18.600
and there are remote communication practices

01:03:18.600 --> 01:03:20.840
and you don't necessarily need to get in a call

01:03:20.840 --> 01:03:23.120
for everything, there are less meetings.

01:03:23.120 --> 01:03:24.240
And if there are meetings

01:03:24.240 --> 01:03:26.560
and there's a proper agenda to those meetings,

01:03:26.560 --> 01:03:28.520
there's proper meeting facilitation.

01:03:28.520 --> 01:03:30.560
As an engineering manager, a lot of times you might,

01:03:30.560 --> 01:03:33.400
you will find that you are facilitating meetings

01:03:33.400 --> 01:03:35.080
and just asking questions

01:03:35.080 --> 01:03:36.600
and asking the right questions

01:03:36.600 --> 01:03:38.360
to get the conversation started

01:03:38.360 --> 01:03:41.280
so that the decisions are being made,

01:03:41.280 --> 01:03:46.000
not every meeting will have the set of questions necessary

01:03:46.000 --> 01:03:49.280
but just having the communication there

01:03:49.280 --> 01:03:50.920
really helps a lot.

01:03:50.920 --> 01:03:53.040
And that you are managing up as well.

01:03:53.040 --> 01:03:54.360
So you have a manager

01:03:54.360 --> 01:03:56.120
and you really need to make sure

01:03:56.120 --> 01:03:57.600
that whatever you're working on,

01:03:57.600 --> 01:04:01.000
you are also sharing the work that you are doing

01:04:01.000 --> 01:04:03.360
and you're truly owning up your career.

01:04:03.360 --> 01:04:05.760
I have written a blog post on managing up

01:04:05.760 --> 01:04:07.040
so take a look at that

01:04:07.040 --> 01:04:08.480
because there's a lot more to talk about

01:04:08.480 --> 01:04:09.320
while managing up

01:04:09.320 --> 01:04:11.320
and why it's so important for your career.

01:04:11.320 --> 01:04:13.400
So make sure you're doing that and communicating that

01:04:13.400 --> 01:04:18.000
because your boss, your manager should be an ally for you

01:04:18.000 --> 01:04:19.640
and they are going to be in rooms

01:04:19.640 --> 01:04:21.520
that probably you're not going to be

01:04:21.520 --> 01:04:23.560
so they need to represent your work

01:04:23.560 --> 01:04:26.560
and that's why you make sure you communicate effectively.

01:04:26.560 --> 01:04:27.720
Then there's stakeholders.

01:04:27.720 --> 01:04:29.840
Now, anytime you're working on something,

01:04:29.840 --> 01:04:31.600
there is a stakeholder for it.

01:04:31.600 --> 01:04:33.800
There are multiple stakeholders responsible

01:04:33.800 --> 01:04:35.280
for the work that you're doing

01:04:35.280 --> 01:04:37.540
and they are impacted, their team is impacted

01:04:37.540 --> 01:04:39.080
with the work you're doing as well.

01:04:39.080 --> 01:04:42.520
So make sure that there are frequent stakeholder updates.

01:04:42.520 --> 01:04:44.720
There's communication with stakeholders.

01:04:44.720 --> 01:04:47.720
They know how frequently you are going to communicate

01:04:47.720 --> 01:04:49.240
and involve them early.

01:04:49.240 --> 01:04:51.320
So if your team was working on something,

01:04:51.320 --> 01:04:53.440
involve them early to get their feedback

01:04:53.440 --> 01:04:56.160
because people are invested in the thing

01:04:56.160 --> 01:04:57.440
that they give feedback on

01:04:57.440 --> 01:05:01.000
and that they put time investment in.

01:05:01.000 --> 01:05:02.840
So if they give feedback early,

01:05:02.840 --> 01:05:04.280
they will be more invested

01:05:04.280 --> 01:05:06.720
and as a result, they are going to be happier

01:05:06.720 --> 01:05:09.880
because they can see that the team is already working on it.

01:05:09.880 --> 01:05:12.520
So this is why communication is extremely important

01:05:12.520 --> 01:05:15.800
with stakeholders and having a really good partnership

01:05:15.800 --> 01:05:17.320
with them and as a manager,

01:05:17.320 --> 01:05:19.840
communication pillar is extremely important

01:05:19.840 --> 01:05:22.360
and if there's one thing you need to start doing

01:05:22.360 --> 01:05:24.400
as you transition from being a developer

01:05:24.400 --> 01:05:26.880
to an engineering manager, it's communication.

01:05:26.880 --> 01:05:29.480
Make sure your communication is on point.

01:05:29.480 --> 01:05:32.920
We talked a lot about communication with stakeholders,

01:05:32.920 --> 01:05:36.000
technical excellence, product, team growth,

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:38.640
people growth and lots more.

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:42.800
But this is a very important bucket as well.

01:05:42.800 --> 01:05:44.760
I've mentioned this in the past,

01:05:44.760 --> 01:05:47.440
in other videos that engineering management

01:05:47.440 --> 01:05:49.840
is just like being on an airplane

01:05:49.840 --> 01:05:53.360
where you need to put your oxygen mask first

01:05:53.360 --> 01:05:56.160
and then give the oxygen mask to your team.

01:05:56.160 --> 01:05:58.320
So if you cannot breathe,

01:05:58.320 --> 01:06:00.560
you will not be able to help your team either.

01:06:00.560 --> 01:06:02.560
So make sure you're investing time

01:06:02.560 --> 01:06:04.760
in your personal growth as well.

01:06:04.760 --> 01:06:06.800
You are leveling up your skills

01:06:06.800 --> 01:06:09.200
and there are a few ways you can level up your skills

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:10.880
through books and eBooks,

01:06:10.880 --> 01:06:12.800
presentations to share learnings,

01:06:12.800 --> 01:06:15.360
book coffee chats with other engineering leaders,

01:06:15.360 --> 01:06:18.560
join communities, take courses and so on.

01:06:18.560 --> 01:06:19.880
And as you already know,

01:06:19.880 --> 01:06:21.320
I'm already working on a course

01:06:21.320 --> 01:06:25.080
on helping you become a successful engineering manager.

01:06:25.080 --> 01:06:27.720
If you're interested, add yourself to the wait list

01:06:27.720 --> 01:06:29.080
and check out the course.

01:06:29.080 --> 01:06:31.040
I'm really excited for this course

01:06:31.040 --> 01:06:33.560
and everything that I'm covering in this specific video

01:06:33.560 --> 01:06:36.600
and lots more is also going to be included.

01:06:36.600 --> 01:06:39.000
And this is the part where you really need

01:06:39.000 --> 01:06:40.400
to level up your skills

01:06:40.400 --> 01:06:42.760
and make sure that the people on your team

01:06:42.760 --> 01:06:44.720
don't become the experiment for your growth.

01:06:44.720 --> 01:06:47.920
So this course is really here to help you for that.

01:06:47.920 --> 01:06:50.040
So the next thing you need to do for your personal growth

01:06:50.040 --> 01:06:51.760
is also document your knowledge,

01:06:51.760 --> 01:06:54.760
share your learning with other leaders in the company,

01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:57.480
make sure that you have a really good relationship

01:06:57.480 --> 01:06:58.560
with them as well,

01:06:58.560 --> 01:07:00.220
because who knows when your team

01:07:00.220 --> 01:07:02.280
would need to work with them,

01:07:02.280 --> 01:07:06.680
but also it's important that having a good network around you

01:07:06.680 --> 01:07:09.680
would really help you grow in your career as well.

01:07:09.680 --> 01:07:12.400
Then set up goals for yourself.

01:07:12.400 --> 01:07:14.500
Again, everything that we talked about

01:07:14.500 --> 01:07:16.120
in the growth section for your team

01:07:16.120 --> 01:07:17.920
also applies to you as well.

01:07:17.920 --> 01:07:20.000
Make sure that you do have a development plan

01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:21.020
that you're working on.

01:07:21.020 --> 01:07:23.160
You are setting up smart goals as well.

01:07:23.160 --> 01:07:26.160
You are creating those goals and measuring as well.

01:07:26.160 --> 01:07:28.400
Because again, as I said before,

01:07:28.400 --> 01:07:30.040
because as an engineering manager,

01:07:30.040 --> 01:07:32.920
it's extremely important that you measure everything.

01:07:32.920 --> 01:07:36.520
So make sure that you are doing that for yourself

01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:39.240
as much as you're doing that for your team as well.

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:40.520
And about all of this,

01:07:40.520 --> 01:07:43.880
it's important that you take care of your mental health

01:07:43.880 --> 01:07:46.980
and personal development as much as you care for your team.

01:07:46.980 --> 01:07:49.320
I really hope you have a solid understanding

01:07:49.320 --> 01:07:53.120
for what it's like to become an engineering manager

01:07:53.120 --> 01:07:55.760
and how that differs from being a developer.

01:07:55.760 --> 01:07:57.860
Just by comparing the day in the life

01:07:57.860 --> 01:07:59.160
of an engineering manager,

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:03.080
we got to know a lot about what a developer does

01:08:03.080 --> 01:08:03.920
and doesn't do,

01:08:03.920 --> 01:08:07.000
and what an engineering manager does behind the scenes.

01:08:07.000 --> 01:08:09.220
You can read all the books on the wall,

01:08:09.220 --> 01:08:12.620
take unlimited workshops on how to become a leader,

01:08:12.620 --> 01:08:15.600
but unless you get practical experience,

01:08:15.600 --> 01:08:19.680
you are never going to know what leadership looks like.

01:08:19.680 --> 01:08:21.220
So in this specific video,

01:08:21.220 --> 01:08:22.680
we are going to talk about

01:08:22.680 --> 01:08:25.400
how you can find opportunities as a leader,

01:08:25.400 --> 01:08:29.200
what does a good leader even mean, or what do they do?

01:08:29.200 --> 01:08:32.720
What are some things you should avoid doing as a leader?

01:08:32.720 --> 01:08:35.680
My name is Ankita, and I am an educator,

01:08:35.680 --> 01:08:38.760
and I will help you level up in your engineering career.

01:08:38.760 --> 01:08:41.720
I have more than a decade worth of experience

01:08:41.720 --> 01:08:44.880
leading developers, so you are in good hands.

01:08:44.880 --> 01:08:46.040
Let's get started.

01:08:46.040 --> 01:08:49.040
So the first thing you should do if you haven't already

01:08:49.040 --> 01:08:51.640
is become a mentor for someone.

01:08:51.640 --> 01:08:54.760
We all have something that we can teach others

01:08:54.760 --> 01:08:56.640
based on our experience.

01:08:56.640 --> 01:08:58.880
So figure out how you can become a mentor

01:08:58.880 --> 01:09:00.680
in the company that you work at,

01:09:00.680 --> 01:09:01.640
or even in general,

01:09:01.640 --> 01:09:04.340
figure out who asks you a bunch of questions

01:09:04.340 --> 01:09:07.320
and see if they're interested in potentially

01:09:07.320 --> 01:09:08.720
being your mentee,

01:09:08.720 --> 01:09:11.400
because being a mentor would be a really good,

01:09:11.400 --> 01:09:13.160
valuable experience for you

01:09:13.160 --> 01:09:15.560
to start tapping into leadership.

01:09:15.560 --> 01:09:18.000
It is kind of like the beginner phase of leadership.

01:09:18.000 --> 01:09:19.900
You will sort of understand

01:09:19.900 --> 01:09:21.920
what it's like to grow someone else.

01:09:21.920 --> 01:09:24.660
You're not directly responsible for their career

01:09:24.660 --> 01:09:26.360
or their growth right now.

01:09:26.360 --> 01:09:28.080
We are still invested in them.

01:09:28.080 --> 01:09:30.440
So you will kind of get an idea

01:09:30.440 --> 01:09:32.520
what it's like to become a leader.

01:09:32.520 --> 01:09:34.200
Next, you should figure out

01:09:34.200 --> 01:09:37.140
if there is a small project that you can lead.

01:09:37.140 --> 01:09:40.280
So again, you can figure out how to work with people,

01:09:40.280 --> 01:09:41.800
how to assign them tasks,

01:09:41.800 --> 01:09:43.920
how to figure out what the priorities are,

01:09:43.920 --> 01:09:46.240
and also work with your direct manager

01:09:46.240 --> 01:09:48.440
as you are in this lead capacity.

01:09:48.440 --> 01:09:51.120
So you will also start to see what your manager does

01:09:51.120 --> 01:09:53.760
in certain situations being a lead.

01:09:53.760 --> 01:09:56.080
So figure out and identify small projects

01:09:56.080 --> 01:09:58.400
that you can work on as a leader.

01:09:58.400 --> 01:10:00.660
Start shadowing your manager in meetings.

01:10:00.660 --> 01:10:02.440
Figure out what decisions they make.

01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:05.920
See what the day in the life of your manager looks like,

01:10:05.920 --> 01:10:09.680
because that's potentially also going to be your day as well.

01:10:09.680 --> 01:10:11.080
If you shadow your manager,

01:10:11.080 --> 01:10:13.720
you can start taking on leadership responsibilities,

01:10:13.720 --> 01:10:15.640
but also figure out if this is something

01:10:15.640 --> 01:10:17.000
that you like or not.

01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:19.380
Because if you don't like it, you don't want to do it,

01:10:19.380 --> 01:10:21.120
that's completely okay.

01:10:21.120 --> 01:10:22.660
But at least by this,

01:10:22.660 --> 01:10:25.880
you would be able to figure out what even a good leader does.

01:10:25.880 --> 01:10:27.920
You become the average of five people

01:10:27.920 --> 01:10:29.240
you surround yourself with.

01:10:29.240 --> 01:10:31.200
If your goal is to become a leader

01:10:31.200 --> 01:10:33.400
or figure out how to become a leader,

01:10:33.400 --> 01:10:38.240
what you can start doing is find out who in the company

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:40.600
is a leader you aspire to become.

01:10:40.600 --> 01:10:42.480
You will learn a lot by watching them,

01:10:42.480 --> 01:10:44.920
and also context is very important.

01:10:44.920 --> 01:10:47.080
So you will figure out what engineering leadership

01:10:47.080 --> 01:10:49.520
looks like in the company that you work at.

01:10:49.520 --> 01:10:53.360
So find a mentor that can help you get there.

01:10:53.360 --> 01:10:56.320
Discuss them your potential goals and your interests,

01:10:56.320 --> 01:10:57.960
and that you're trying to figure out

01:10:57.960 --> 01:11:00.040
if you want to become a leader or not.

01:11:00.040 --> 01:11:03.240
A leader in one company might struggle in another.

01:11:03.240 --> 01:11:05.120
That's why finding a mentor in your org

01:11:05.120 --> 01:11:08.040
would really help you speed up that learning process.

01:11:08.040 --> 01:11:09.700
So what makes a good leader?

01:11:09.700 --> 01:11:12.040
If you want to become one, you need to figure out

01:11:12.040 --> 01:11:14.880
what is the secret sauce to becoming a leader?

01:11:14.880 --> 01:11:17.120
Now, there's never a checklist of things

01:11:17.120 --> 01:11:20.400
that I can provide and say, yeah, check this off

01:11:20.400 --> 01:11:22.080
and you will become a good leader.

01:11:22.080 --> 01:11:24.840
Leadership is complex because we are dealing with people

01:11:24.840 --> 01:11:26.680
and people are humans.

01:11:26.680 --> 01:11:29.560
So you need to tweak your checklist

01:11:29.560 --> 01:11:31.920
and add to it or remove items from it

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:33.900
depending on the org you work at.

01:11:33.900 --> 01:11:36.280
But here are a few things that makes a good leader.

01:11:36.280 --> 01:11:40.360
You will notice that the people that work for that leader

01:11:40.360 --> 01:11:41.800
are incredibly happy.

01:11:41.800 --> 01:11:44.200
Or even if they are not very happy,

01:11:44.200 --> 01:11:48.440
they are satisfied with their job and job responsibilities.

01:11:48.440 --> 01:11:49.980
A good leader will always know

01:11:49.980 --> 01:11:51.960
how to build a high-performing team.

01:11:51.960 --> 01:11:55.640
And a high-performing team is not a team of high performers.

01:11:55.640 --> 01:11:58.200
A high-performing team is essentially a team

01:11:58.200 --> 01:12:00.400
that collaborates effectively,

01:12:00.400 --> 01:12:02.960
knows how to give feedback to each other,

01:12:02.960 --> 01:12:06.000
they ship fast, they iterate, and they move on.

01:12:06.000 --> 01:12:07.640
A good leader will know how to create

01:12:07.640 --> 01:12:10.600
a high-performing team out of any situation.

01:12:10.600 --> 01:12:14.440
A good leader is not just growing the people on their team,

01:12:14.440 --> 01:12:16.400
but they are also driving business results,

01:12:16.400 --> 01:12:18.040
which are very, very important.

01:12:18.040 --> 01:12:19.880
You can think of this analogy

01:12:19.880 --> 01:12:23.200
where you can imagine a duck swimming in a pond.

01:12:23.200 --> 01:12:26.040
When you look at that duck, the duck seems really calm

01:12:26.040 --> 01:12:28.160
and everything is going okay.

01:12:28.160 --> 01:12:31.240
But underwater, you will notice that the duck

01:12:31.240 --> 01:12:35.600
is working really hard to make sure that they seem calm

01:12:35.600 --> 01:12:37.120
on the surface level.

01:12:37.120 --> 01:12:40.760
And that's exactly what a good leader would look like.

01:12:40.760 --> 01:12:43.400
You will not notice that there's a lot of work

01:12:43.400 --> 01:12:45.360
that's going on in the background

01:12:45.360 --> 01:12:48.440
because they're doing such a good job shielding the team

01:12:48.440 --> 01:12:51.800
from all the chaos and pressure that's happening in the org.

01:12:51.800 --> 01:12:55.880
So a good leader will definitely be doing that.

01:12:55.880 --> 01:12:58.920
A good leader will know exactly how to grow people

01:12:58.920 --> 01:13:00.240
at different skill levels.

01:13:00.240 --> 01:13:02.920
And if they don't know, then they're going to ask others

01:13:02.920 --> 01:13:06.480
for help and figure out how you can grow in your team.

01:13:06.480 --> 01:13:08.720
They are never going to give you the answers.

01:13:08.720 --> 01:13:12.080
They're always going to coach you and question you

01:13:12.080 --> 01:13:14.640
so that you can come to those answers yourself.

01:13:14.640 --> 01:13:18.280
And it always creates that light bulb moment for you.

01:13:18.280 --> 01:13:20.520
A good leader will not give you the answers

01:13:20.520 --> 01:13:21.840
because they know that in the future,

01:13:21.840 --> 01:13:24.320
you're going to come back to them with the same question.

01:13:24.320 --> 01:13:26.080
But if they ask you, then you will come

01:13:26.080 --> 01:13:27.600
to that answer yourself

01:13:27.600 --> 01:13:29.720
and you don't need to ask them in the future.

01:13:29.720 --> 01:13:31.440
And that's what a good leader does.

01:13:31.440 --> 01:13:34.480
Communication is extremely important as a leader

01:13:34.480 --> 01:13:38.120
and a good leader will always communicate effectively,

01:13:38.120 --> 01:13:41.040
thoughtfully and will prepare the communication

01:13:41.040 --> 01:13:43.760
before any major event.

01:13:43.760 --> 01:13:45.640
So for example, there might be different types

01:13:45.640 --> 01:13:46.560
of communication.

01:13:46.560 --> 01:13:48.200
There could be one-on-one communication

01:13:48.200 --> 01:13:50.920
with the direct reports or people they didn't work with

01:13:50.920 --> 01:13:52.920
or they could be org-wide communication

01:13:52.920 --> 01:13:54.560
or big channel communication.

01:13:54.560 --> 01:13:57.360
And a good leader will always thoughtfully prepare

01:13:57.360 --> 01:13:59.040
what communication looks like.

01:13:59.040 --> 01:14:02.240
This communication can literally make or break a company.

01:14:02.240 --> 01:14:05.320
It can create chaos and it will create uncertainty

01:14:05.320 --> 01:14:08.080
and you don't really want that.

01:14:08.080 --> 01:14:09.520
So if you want to be a good leader,

01:14:09.520 --> 01:14:11.600
you need to make sure that you're communicating

01:14:11.600 --> 01:14:13.320
thoughtfully and effectively.

01:14:13.320 --> 01:14:16.240
A good strategy for this is always create a document

01:14:16.240 --> 01:14:18.600
and write down everything that you want to say

01:14:18.600 --> 01:14:23.280
and review it and tweak it and use your words wisely

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:26.880
and then get other leaders to review the document as well

01:14:26.880 --> 01:14:29.400
before sharing it in a channel

01:14:29.400 --> 01:14:31.520
because the impact of that could be huge.

01:14:31.520 --> 01:14:34.520
And this way you are aligned on the communication.

01:14:34.520 --> 01:14:36.240
So a good leader will always have

01:14:36.240 --> 01:14:38.560
really good communication strategies.

01:14:38.560 --> 01:14:41.760
I have a video coming out on how to effectively communicate

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:42.760
that you should check out.

01:14:42.760 --> 01:14:46.640
A good leader will always create psychological safety.

01:14:46.640 --> 01:14:50.080
Now, psychological safety means that you can make mistakes

01:14:50.080 --> 01:14:53.720
as a developer or as a member of the team

01:14:53.720 --> 01:14:55.360
and you won't be punished for it.

01:14:55.360 --> 01:14:57.880
There are no bad consequences for it.

01:14:57.880 --> 01:15:02.400
As a developer, you are going to make a lot of mistakes.

01:15:02.400 --> 01:15:06.960
So imagine if your manager or your leader

01:15:06.960 --> 01:15:10.560
doesn't create a psychologically safe place

01:15:10.560 --> 01:15:14.760
to make mistakes, then you are going to have a ton of bugs

01:15:14.760 --> 01:15:17.400
and you're always going to hide your mistakes.

01:15:17.400 --> 01:15:18.840
And if you want to be a good leader,

01:15:18.840 --> 01:15:20.000
you should never do that.

01:15:20.000 --> 01:15:22.160
So a good leader will always create

01:15:22.160 --> 01:15:25.880
a psychologically safe place for you to make mistakes

01:15:25.880 --> 01:15:28.200
and won't punish you for it.

01:15:28.200 --> 01:15:32.560
Instead, figure out a way to avoid those same mistakes

01:15:32.560 --> 01:15:34.640
in the future and create a process for it.

01:15:34.640 --> 01:15:39.120
As a good leader, you must always praise publicly

01:15:39.120 --> 01:15:40.880
and give feedback privately.

01:15:40.880 --> 01:15:42.720
So this is a perfect example

01:15:42.720 --> 01:15:45.560
wherein if you want to create a psychologically safe place,

01:15:45.560 --> 01:15:47.560
never bash anyone publicly

01:15:47.560 --> 01:15:49.680
because you're going to create that fear

01:15:49.680 --> 01:15:51.280
for everyone else on your team

01:15:51.280 --> 01:15:52.600
and you really don't want to do that.

01:15:52.600 --> 01:15:55.160
A good leader will create that safe space

01:15:55.160 --> 01:15:56.880
for everyone to make mistakes.

01:15:56.880 --> 01:15:58.840
As a good leader, you always want your people

01:15:58.840 --> 01:16:00.200
to grow and level up.

01:16:00.200 --> 01:16:04.200
If they are stagnated, if they're not growing,

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:05.640
then what's the point?

01:16:05.640 --> 01:16:08.040
You need to make sure that the people on your team

01:16:08.040 --> 01:16:10.720
are growing and this will really make you a good leader

01:16:10.720 --> 01:16:12.240
because you are helping them grow.

01:16:12.240 --> 01:16:13.560
You're giving them feedback,

01:16:13.560 --> 01:16:16.680
you are creating a development plan for them,

01:16:16.680 --> 01:16:18.640
you're really figuring out the gaps they have

01:16:18.640 --> 01:16:20.400
and helping them get to the goal

01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:21.720
where they want to get to,

01:16:21.720 --> 01:16:23.680
or you're helping them level up.

01:16:23.680 --> 01:16:25.600
So really you need to make sure

01:16:25.600 --> 01:16:27.480
that the people on your team are growing

01:16:27.480 --> 01:16:29.240
as a really good leader.

01:16:29.240 --> 01:16:32.120
You don't need to problem solve each and everything

01:16:32.120 --> 01:16:34.200
as you did as a developer.

01:16:34.200 --> 01:16:37.800
Now you need to handle problems on a case by case basis

01:16:37.800 --> 01:16:41.560
because these problems are often problems with people.

01:16:41.560 --> 01:16:43.680
So a good leader will know exactly

01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:47.200
how to handle a specific difficult situation.

01:16:47.200 --> 01:16:48.520
You never want to run away

01:16:48.520 --> 01:16:50.560
from difficult situations as a leader

01:16:50.560 --> 01:16:53.280
and you should almost be comfortable dealing with it.

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:56.040
It can be hard at first, but the more you do it,

01:16:56.040 --> 01:16:58.920
the better it get, but handle with care

01:16:58.920 --> 01:17:00.840
and handle with thoughtfulness

01:17:00.840 --> 01:17:02.680
and it will always come across as genuine.

01:17:02.680 --> 01:17:05.240
So as a leader, you always want to analyze things

01:17:05.240 --> 01:17:06.720
on a case by case basis

01:17:06.720 --> 01:17:10.600
and figure out problems or brainstorm problems together.

01:17:10.600 --> 01:17:13.000
You might have a lot of control as a leader

01:17:13.000 --> 01:17:14.960
and a lot of power as a leader,

01:17:14.960 --> 01:17:17.640
but you still want people to come work for you.

01:17:17.640 --> 01:17:22.600
And if you act like a boss and not as a leader,

01:17:22.600 --> 01:17:27.320
then you will not have those people coming to work for you.

01:17:27.320 --> 01:17:28.960
And you really don't want to do that

01:17:28.960 --> 01:17:30.800
because you will not have a team.

01:17:30.800 --> 01:17:34.760
So really make sure that you don't act like a boss,

01:17:34.760 --> 01:17:38.240
but you start being a leader, start acting like a leader.

01:17:38.240 --> 01:17:40.880
And based on some of the things I talked about,

01:17:40.880 --> 01:17:42.560
because if you start being a boss,

01:17:42.560 --> 01:17:43.720
then you're commanding

01:17:43.720 --> 01:17:46.440
and people don't like to work for bosses.

01:17:46.440 --> 01:17:48.760
So start being a good leader.

01:17:48.760 --> 01:17:51.120
You need to lead by example as a leader.

01:17:51.120 --> 01:17:53.640
You need to have a positive influence on others.

01:17:53.640 --> 01:17:55.400
By growing other people,

01:17:55.400 --> 01:17:57.920
you are going to grow yourself as well

01:17:57.920 --> 01:18:00.720
and you're going to level up your skills as well.

01:18:00.720 --> 01:18:04.080
Now, if you want people to follow you

01:18:04.080 --> 01:18:06.360
and if you really need to walk the talk,

01:18:06.360 --> 01:18:09.440
if you want people to follow the same guidelines

01:18:09.440 --> 01:18:12.000
and rules that you have set for them.

01:18:12.000 --> 01:18:15.360
So for example, if you don't want people to work late,

01:18:15.360 --> 01:18:17.600
then don't work late as well.

01:18:17.600 --> 01:18:21.280
If you don't want people to DM each other

01:18:21.280 --> 01:18:25.120
or message in channels after work hours,

01:18:25.120 --> 01:18:29.320
then don't message in work channels or be online

01:18:29.320 --> 01:18:32.120
or show them that you are online after work.

01:18:32.120 --> 01:18:34.440
Because again, people are just going to follow you

01:18:34.440 --> 01:18:35.600
because you're the leader.

01:18:35.600 --> 01:18:37.120
So whether you like it or not,

01:18:37.120 --> 01:18:39.520
the behaviors that you adopt as a leader

01:18:39.520 --> 01:18:43.000
are something that is going to be easily trickled down

01:18:43.000 --> 01:18:45.520
to all your team members.

01:18:45.520 --> 01:18:48.920
Because as a lead, you're setting the culture of your team.

01:18:48.920 --> 01:18:50.040
So really you need to make sure

01:18:50.040 --> 01:18:51.720
that you're leading by example.

01:18:51.720 --> 01:18:55.400
When people say that they love or hate the company

01:18:55.400 --> 01:18:58.560
they work for, you can be 90% confident

01:18:58.560 --> 01:19:00.560
that they're talking about their manager

01:19:00.560 --> 01:19:05.160
because a manager can really make or break someone's career.

01:19:05.160 --> 01:19:06.560
So you really need to make sure

01:19:06.560 --> 01:19:09.260
that you are being a good leader

01:19:09.260 --> 01:19:11.440
and setting a good example for them.

01:19:11.440 --> 01:19:13.580
I have mentioned a million different things

01:19:13.580 --> 01:19:15.240
for what makes a good leader

01:19:15.240 --> 01:19:18.280
and I can say a million more things more.

01:19:18.280 --> 01:19:21.280
But again, as I mentioned at the beginning of this video,

01:19:21.280 --> 01:19:22.440
you really need to make sure

01:19:22.440 --> 01:19:25.460
that you are gaining that practical experience,

01:19:25.460 --> 01:19:27.640
finding those opportunities for yourself

01:19:27.640 --> 01:19:31.680
in order to hopefully become a leader in the future.

01:19:31.680 --> 01:19:33.280
Hard skills get you hired,

01:19:33.280 --> 01:19:35.160
but soft skills get you promoted.

01:19:35.160 --> 01:19:37.780
If you had to pick one soft skill

01:19:37.780 --> 01:19:39.440
that you truly need to master,

01:19:39.440 --> 01:19:41.640
then it has to be communication.

01:19:41.640 --> 01:19:44.060
After leading 100 plus developers and leaders,

01:19:44.060 --> 01:19:47.640
I can assure you that folks that knew how to communicate,

01:19:47.640 --> 01:19:49.600
grew quickly, got promoted

01:19:49.600 --> 01:19:52.960
and truly created that impact that lasted for years.

01:19:52.960 --> 01:19:55.120
If you are interested in leveling up

01:19:55.120 --> 01:19:57.560
and getting promoted, then keep watching.

01:19:57.560 --> 01:20:00.740
In this video, I'm going to share a four step,

01:20:00.740 --> 01:20:04.880
simple formula for you to create a communication strategy

01:20:04.880 --> 01:20:07.520
that you can apply in any type of meeting.

01:20:07.520 --> 01:20:08.880
So let's get started.

01:20:08.880 --> 01:20:10.360
So the first step in this formula

01:20:10.360 --> 01:20:13.320
is identifying who you're communicating with.

01:20:13.320 --> 01:20:15.200
Knowing who you're going to communicate with

01:20:15.200 --> 01:20:17.280
is going to be a game changer for you.

01:20:17.280 --> 01:20:18.120
You know why?

01:20:18.120 --> 01:20:20.400
Because after you know who they are,

01:20:20.400 --> 01:20:23.560
you can share more or less context in that meeting.

01:20:23.560 --> 01:20:25.300
Before you get into that meeting,

01:20:25.300 --> 01:20:27.360
figure out who's going to attend the meeting,

01:20:27.360 --> 01:20:29.720
what their roles are, what do they care about,

01:20:29.720 --> 01:20:30.980
what their objectives are,

01:20:30.980 --> 01:20:33.960
and how do they tie into the context of the meeting

01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:36.640
and why were they invited to begin with.

01:20:36.640 --> 01:20:39.540
Knowing that is going to be huge for you

01:20:39.540 --> 01:20:41.720
because then you can accordingly decide

01:20:41.720 --> 01:20:43.880
whether you should provide more context,

01:20:43.880 --> 01:20:45.800
whether you should ask them more questions

01:20:45.800 --> 01:20:47.680
or how they're going to help your team.

01:20:47.680 --> 01:20:50.360
This is going to make you look more confident

01:20:50.360 --> 01:20:53.400
because you are, because you have prepped for that meeting.

01:20:53.400 --> 01:20:55.980
So identifying who you're going to communicate with

01:20:55.980 --> 01:20:57.840
is going to be a game changer.

01:20:57.840 --> 01:21:01.360
Unfortunately, 90% of the folks don't know

01:21:01.360 --> 01:21:03.040
which meeting they're getting into.

01:21:03.040 --> 01:21:06.040
They wait for the calendar tool to notify them

01:21:06.040 --> 01:21:07.920
five minutes before the meeting,

01:21:07.920 --> 01:21:09.880
and then they decide to go into that meeting,

01:21:09.880 --> 01:21:12.160
not knowing the purpose of the meeting,

01:21:12.160 --> 01:21:13.600
who's attending the meeting,

01:21:13.600 --> 01:21:16.580
and then that completely slows down the meeting.

01:21:16.580 --> 01:21:20.000
So in order to craft a really good communication strategy,

01:21:20.000 --> 01:21:21.960
you need to do the opposite of that.

01:21:21.960 --> 01:21:24.300
You need to identify who you're talking to

01:21:24.300 --> 01:21:26.640
so you know how to best position yourself.

01:21:27.700 --> 01:21:31.160
The second part of the formula is to focus on the outcome

01:21:31.160 --> 01:21:33.080
that you would like to get to.

01:21:33.080 --> 01:21:36.940
Now, hold on a second and let's take a step back here.

01:21:36.940 --> 01:21:40.360
Focusing on the outcome is so important.

01:21:40.360 --> 01:21:42.920
If you know what you want to get out of that meeting

01:21:42.920 --> 01:21:44.900
before you even get into it,

01:21:44.900 --> 01:21:48.180
or what you want to get out of that discussion,

01:21:48.180 --> 01:21:53.260
then you can basically backtrack and figure out exactly

01:21:53.260 --> 01:21:54.640
what questions you want to ask

01:21:54.640 --> 01:21:58.000
and how you want to position that meeting.

01:21:58.000 --> 01:22:01.280
A lot of times we get too invested in the solutions

01:22:01.280 --> 01:22:03.760
or we are too passionate about the problem

01:22:03.760 --> 01:22:05.760
that we figured out what exactly

01:22:05.760 --> 01:22:08.760
we want to get out of the discussion with anyone.

01:22:08.760 --> 01:22:12.880
So if you figure out what exact outcome you want to get to,

01:22:12.880 --> 01:22:15.180
then you can backtrack and craft steps

01:22:15.180 --> 01:22:16.940
for how you want to get to that outcome.

01:22:16.940 --> 01:22:17.960
If you get into the meeting

01:22:17.960 --> 01:22:20.640
and you realize that this meeting is not productive

01:22:20.640 --> 01:22:23.820
and you will not get closer to the outcome,

01:22:23.820 --> 01:22:26.120
in fact, you are getting derailed

01:22:26.120 --> 01:22:28.240
by a completely different conversation,

01:22:28.240 --> 01:22:30.960
then backtrack and figure out what your outcome is

01:22:30.960 --> 01:22:33.200
and remind your team of that outcome.

01:22:33.200 --> 01:22:36.040
So for example, if you get into a heated discussion

01:22:36.040 --> 01:22:39.640
on architectural decision with another teammate,

01:22:39.640 --> 01:22:41.080
instead of getting too heated up,

01:22:41.080 --> 01:22:42.840
try to figure out what is the outcome

01:22:42.840 --> 01:22:45.080
you would like to get to based on your discussion.

01:22:45.080 --> 01:22:48.760
Now the outcome is that you need to agree upon

01:22:48.760 --> 01:22:51.840
a common solution that works for your team

01:22:51.840 --> 01:22:54.640
and for the business and for your company, right?

01:22:54.640 --> 01:22:55.920
That is the outcome.

01:22:55.920 --> 01:22:59.100
Then you shouldn't get too hung up on having opinions

01:22:59.100 --> 01:23:02.600
that are not supporting that specific outcome.

01:23:02.600 --> 01:23:05.120
So again, if you do that and backtrack it,

01:23:05.120 --> 01:23:07.280
that will really help you understand

01:23:07.280 --> 01:23:08.920
where you would like to get to.

01:23:08.920 --> 01:23:12.100
Now, whatever strategy that we are discussing right now,

01:23:12.100 --> 01:23:14.880
the four-step formula along with examples,

01:23:14.880 --> 01:23:16.240
if you'd like a template for it,

01:23:16.240 --> 01:23:18.600
then take a look in the description below

01:23:18.600 --> 01:23:20.520
and download the template for free.

01:23:24.560 --> 01:23:27.720
Now, the third part of the formula is what impact

01:23:27.720 --> 01:23:30.340
you would like to create from this specific meeting.

01:23:30.340 --> 01:23:32.400
Now, in order to make sure that you're creating impact

01:23:32.400 --> 01:23:33.640
as part of every meeting,

01:23:33.640 --> 01:23:36.040
you need to create a well-structured meeting.

01:23:36.040 --> 01:23:39.320
So for example, before you even get to the meeting,

01:23:39.320 --> 01:23:42.200
make sure that every meeting has a clear agenda

01:23:42.200 --> 01:23:44.920
and whatever outcome that we have just talked about

01:23:44.920 --> 01:23:47.640
is also documented in that specific meeting.

01:23:47.640 --> 01:23:50.760
So people are aware what to discuss upon.

01:23:50.760 --> 01:23:52.520
If the meeting gets derailed,

01:23:52.520 --> 01:23:55.400
make sure that people discuss the main topic

01:23:55.400 --> 01:23:57.880
of that specific meeting and discussion.

01:23:57.880 --> 01:23:59.960
Engage participants in the meeting.

01:23:59.960 --> 01:24:03.680
For example, you know that you need marketing's input,

01:24:03.680 --> 01:24:07.500
or if you need a specific developer's input in that meeting,

01:24:07.500 --> 01:24:09.520
make sure that you get asked that input

01:24:09.520 --> 01:24:11.840
and make it a engaging meeting

01:24:11.840 --> 01:24:14.920
so that it's not just you who's making the decision,

01:24:14.920 --> 01:24:17.360
but it's the entire team or the entire group

01:24:17.360 --> 01:24:18.840
that's invited to the meeting,

01:24:18.840 --> 01:24:21.520
really thinking out loud and giving that opinion.

01:24:21.520 --> 01:24:25.760
Because again, in order to make sure that you level up

01:24:25.760 --> 01:24:27.760
and get promoted and really come across

01:24:27.760 --> 01:24:29.480
as a confident communicator,

01:24:29.480 --> 01:24:31.520
you really don't wanna do this on your own.

01:24:31.520 --> 01:24:33.800
You really wanna bring the entire group together

01:24:33.800 --> 01:24:35.600
to do this together.

01:24:35.600 --> 01:24:37.120
And manage time effectively.

01:24:37.120 --> 01:24:38.800
Time is very important.

01:24:38.800 --> 01:24:40.800
If you make sure that the meeting starts on time

01:24:40.800 --> 01:24:41.880
and ends on time,

01:24:41.880 --> 01:24:44.840
that is going to be really, really important for everyone

01:24:44.840 --> 01:24:47.120
because again, time is energy.

01:24:47.120 --> 01:24:48.760
The more time you spend on something,

01:24:48.760 --> 01:24:50.800
the more energy you're going to spend on something.

01:24:50.800 --> 01:24:51.960
So you really wanna make sure

01:24:51.960 --> 01:24:54.740
that you are managing the meeting right

01:24:54.740 --> 01:24:57.480
and making sure that it starts on time and ends on time.

01:24:57.480 --> 01:25:01.480
If you are getting into a meeting or a discussion,

01:25:01.480 --> 01:25:04.200
you need to think about what should people remember

01:25:04.200 --> 01:25:07.080
about you as they walk away from that meeting.

01:25:07.080 --> 01:25:07.920
So for example,

01:25:07.920 --> 01:25:11.000
if you're really trying to create a brand around GraphQL

01:25:11.000 --> 01:25:12.680
and you really want people to remember

01:25:12.680 --> 01:25:15.640
that you are a GraphQL expert

01:25:15.640 --> 01:25:17.800
or you really know a lot about GraphQL,

01:25:17.800 --> 01:25:19.680
then before you get into that meeting,

01:25:19.680 --> 01:25:21.680
prep for that meeting and really note down

01:25:21.680 --> 01:25:23.160
what are the different types of questions

01:25:23.160 --> 01:25:25.800
that are going to be asked as part of that discussion

01:25:25.800 --> 01:25:27.680
and answer them and help the team.

01:25:27.680 --> 01:25:30.440
Even if you don't know the answer, that's completely okay.

01:25:30.440 --> 01:25:32.040
You can take away those questions

01:25:32.040 --> 01:25:35.400
and really figure out how to help the team later on.

01:25:35.400 --> 01:25:38.560
But again, make sure that you know what impact

01:25:38.560 --> 01:25:40.800
you would like to create as part of that meeting.

01:25:40.800 --> 01:25:42.800
The minute you start thinking about that,

01:25:42.800 --> 01:25:46.320
you're going to behave a bit differently than you do.

01:25:46.320 --> 01:25:48.880
You're going to start being more active in the meeting.

01:25:48.880 --> 01:25:50.720
You're going to be an important part of the meeting,

01:25:50.720 --> 01:25:52.640
which is really, really important.

01:25:52.640 --> 01:25:56.320
If you are interested in knowing how to be a great leader

01:25:56.320 --> 01:25:58.520
and in fact, how to be a badass one,

01:25:58.520 --> 01:26:01.720
then check out another video that I have created.

01:26:01.720 --> 01:26:03.760
The fourth step in the formula is

01:26:03.760 --> 01:26:05.920
what are some of the actions that you can create

01:26:05.920 --> 01:26:08.160
as part of this specific discussion?

01:26:08.160 --> 01:26:09.440
Towards the end of every meeting

01:26:09.440 --> 01:26:11.080
or towards the end of every discussion,

01:26:11.080 --> 01:26:14.080
there are a list of action items that you can take away

01:26:14.080 --> 01:26:15.240
that you can work on.

01:26:15.240 --> 01:26:17.400
You can remind the group of that.

01:26:17.400 --> 01:26:19.460
You can remind the team of that,

01:26:19.460 --> 01:26:21.920
but you can also take away some of those action items

01:26:21.920 --> 01:26:23.800
and make sure that they are delivered.

01:26:23.800 --> 01:26:25.080
Once they are done,

01:26:25.080 --> 01:26:27.700
let the team know in the channels that you're part of,

01:26:27.700 --> 01:26:29.280
where all your other team members are,

01:26:29.280 --> 01:26:31.500
because again, that's going to make you look

01:26:31.500 --> 01:26:34.560
a lot more confident and you are communicating seamlessly.

01:26:34.560 --> 01:26:36.400
What action items can you take

01:26:36.400 --> 01:26:38.840
from that specific discussion or meeting?

01:26:38.840 --> 01:26:40.840
Make sure that it's documented somewhere

01:26:40.840 --> 01:26:44.440
so that everyone knows what you have discussed.

01:26:44.440 --> 01:26:47.480
There's no room for miscommunication and misunderstanding

01:26:47.480 --> 01:26:49.320
because everything is documented.

01:26:49.320 --> 01:26:51.600
Make sure that you get alignment from everyone else

01:26:51.600 --> 01:26:54.760
by repeating the goal of the meeting

01:26:54.760 --> 01:26:57.440
and the action items that came out of that meeting

01:26:57.440 --> 01:26:59.920
because you are going to look more confident and prepared

01:26:59.920 --> 01:27:02.440
and you know exactly everyone is aligned

01:27:02.440 --> 01:27:03.800
on that specific topic.

01:27:03.800 --> 01:27:06.520
And overall, just be helpful in making sure

01:27:06.520 --> 01:27:09.680
that discussion gets to the specific outcome

01:27:09.680 --> 01:27:11.200
that you would like to get to.

01:27:12.160 --> 01:27:16.280
The key thing here is that throughout this specific video

01:27:16.280 --> 01:27:17.920
and in this specific formula,

01:27:17.920 --> 01:27:19.860
I have talked about all the different ways

01:27:19.860 --> 01:27:22.240
you can communicate how you need to be prepared

01:27:22.240 --> 01:27:24.800
for the meeting, how you need to make sure

01:27:24.800 --> 01:27:26.400
that you're answering questions,

01:27:26.400 --> 01:27:28.600
how you can share context and so on.

01:27:28.600 --> 01:27:29.800
And all of these things,

01:27:29.800 --> 01:27:32.140
if you keep repeating over and over,

01:27:32.140 --> 01:27:34.240
you are going to come across as really confident

01:27:34.240 --> 01:27:36.960
because you are someone who is prepared for the meeting.

01:27:36.960 --> 01:27:39.680
You know exactly what the discussion is about

01:27:39.680 --> 01:27:43.380
so you know that you have that additional leverage

01:27:43.380 --> 01:27:45.680
over others in that specific meeting.

01:27:45.680 --> 01:27:48.880
This lesson, we are going to talk about some frameworks

01:27:48.880 --> 01:27:53.280
that we can use to learn how to give feedback effectively

01:27:53.280 --> 01:27:55.560
and the importance of feedback overall.

01:27:57.060 --> 01:28:00.200
Now, feedback is so personal and unique

01:28:00.200 --> 01:28:03.680
that you would need to keep evolving your framework.

01:28:03.680 --> 01:28:06.980
You will learn that the feedback you may give to one person

01:28:06.980 --> 01:28:08.520
is completely different to the feedback

01:28:08.520 --> 01:28:09.860
you share with someone else.

01:28:09.860 --> 01:28:12.360
So feedback is so personal and so unique

01:28:12.360 --> 01:28:14.120
that you need to keep evolving it.

01:28:16.040 --> 01:28:18.480
And we have talked about the buddy pattern

01:28:18.480 --> 01:28:20.340
in the anti-patterns lesson

01:28:20.340 --> 01:28:23.480
and you wanna be liked and loved so badly

01:28:23.480 --> 01:28:25.120
that you will never give a single piece

01:28:25.120 --> 01:28:26.280
of negative feedback.

01:28:26.280 --> 01:28:28.160
And we talked about this as a buddy pattern

01:28:28.160 --> 01:28:31.760
in the anti-patterns lesson of engineering leadership.

01:28:31.760 --> 01:28:33.640
You wanna be liked, we wanna be loved

01:28:33.640 --> 01:28:35.520
because we are humans after all

01:28:35.520 --> 01:28:38.760
and we never wanna do the hard things of giving feedback.

01:28:38.760 --> 01:28:41.400
But this behavior is not going to create

01:28:41.400 --> 01:28:43.440
any positive team culture.

01:28:43.440 --> 01:28:46.200
In fact, it's going to do the opposite of that.

01:28:46.200 --> 01:28:48.300
You're going to slow down, you're learning,

01:28:48.300 --> 01:28:49.960
you're going to slow down your team

01:28:49.960 --> 01:28:51.380
because you're in danger,

01:28:51.380 --> 01:28:55.640
because this might lead to more potential behavior

01:28:55.640 --> 01:28:57.920
which might be worse for your team.

01:28:57.920 --> 01:29:00.560
You really need to make sure that you give feedback.

01:29:00.560 --> 01:29:04.160
And feedback is the best gift you can give it to someone.

01:29:04.160 --> 01:29:07.320
Most leaders are scared to give feedback

01:29:07.320 --> 01:29:09.760
because they think that their colleague

01:29:09.760 --> 01:29:12.280
might not take them in the right way.

01:29:12.280 --> 01:29:13.840
The colleague might get defensive,

01:29:13.840 --> 01:29:16.640
get mad at you for sharing feedback,

01:29:16.640 --> 01:29:20.040
but it is the best gift you can give it to someone.

01:29:20.040 --> 01:29:22.160
Negative feedback is difficult.

01:29:22.160 --> 01:29:25.560
You may find excuses to not do it or postpone it,

01:29:25.560 --> 01:29:27.140
but keep this in mind,

01:29:27.140 --> 01:29:29.320
that if you're not sharing this feedback,

01:29:29.320 --> 01:29:32.260
you give them zero chance of improvement

01:29:32.260 --> 01:29:34.160
and that is not fair to them.

01:29:34.160 --> 01:29:37.800
So giving negative feedback is extremely important.

01:29:37.800 --> 01:29:40.260
And the problem is never the person themselves,

01:29:40.260 --> 01:29:42.480
it's the behavior that they're exhibiting.

01:29:42.480 --> 01:29:44.680
And if you know this about feedback,

01:29:44.680 --> 01:29:47.560
that it's not the person, it's not personal,

01:29:47.560 --> 01:29:49.960
it's the behavior that we need to rectify,

01:29:49.960 --> 01:29:53.000
suddenly a lot of things change when you're giving feedback.

01:29:54.360 --> 01:29:59.240
And if you combine that with our initial fight

01:29:59.240 --> 01:30:01.820
or flight response, then it gets worse.

01:30:01.820 --> 01:30:03.880
You're likely to experience a social threat

01:30:03.880 --> 01:30:07.000
when facing people of higher status,

01:30:07.000 --> 01:30:09.480
feeling or sense of uncertainty,

01:30:09.480 --> 01:30:10.840
or feeling disconnected,

01:30:10.840 --> 01:30:13.000
which is something that is unfair.

01:30:13.000 --> 01:30:16.640
Now, if you combine that with like fire or flight response,

01:30:16.640 --> 01:30:19.680
it's no wonder feedback is not part of our high priority list

01:30:19.680 --> 01:30:21.760
because whenever we receive feedback,

01:30:21.760 --> 01:30:23.280
we get really defensive.

01:30:23.280 --> 01:30:25.580
And that's not what we want, but that's what happens.

01:30:25.580 --> 01:30:28.660
And that's why feedback is difficult to receive and to give.

01:30:30.200 --> 01:30:33.080
But one hot tip is that you don't give feedback

01:30:33.080 --> 01:30:36.200
or take any action that will impact others

01:30:36.200 --> 01:30:38.880
if you don't have the time to address the feedback,

01:30:38.880 --> 01:30:40.820
or if you don't have time to listen to their story,

01:30:40.820 --> 01:30:42.460
that is if you're in a rush.

01:30:42.460 --> 01:30:44.920
Because if you're in a rush to give someone the feedback,

01:30:44.920 --> 01:30:46.680
it's a sign that you have failed them

01:30:46.680 --> 01:30:49.080
before you even got started with them.

01:30:49.080 --> 01:30:51.920
It's best to hold off until you have something concrete

01:30:51.920 --> 01:30:53.000
or positive to say,

01:30:53.000 --> 01:30:55.400
and have more something constructive to say.

01:30:55.400 --> 01:30:57.320
Because when you're feeling like you have to give someone

01:30:57.320 --> 01:30:59.480
feedback and you cannot wait until tomorrow,

01:30:59.480 --> 01:31:02.580
it's a sign that maybe there's a problem with you,

01:31:02.580 --> 01:31:06.560
you are insecure or something that has gone wrong with you,

01:31:06.560 --> 01:31:09.220
or they had a really massive impact on you

01:31:09.220 --> 01:31:11.800
with the behavior that they exhibited.

01:31:11.800 --> 01:31:14.520
So make sure that you take the time, take the day off,

01:31:14.520 --> 01:31:18.140
don't stress too much, and after you give it some time,

01:31:18.140 --> 01:31:21.120
collect your thoughts and give them feedback.

01:31:21.120 --> 01:31:24.020
And reflect when you felt grateful

01:31:24.020 --> 01:31:25.080
after receiving feedback.

01:31:25.080 --> 01:31:28.200
So think of a time when someone gave you feedback

01:31:28.200 --> 01:31:30.280
that actually made you feel good,

01:31:30.280 --> 01:31:32.440
even though it was constructive feedback.

01:31:32.440 --> 01:31:34.600
How did they convey their message?

01:31:34.600 --> 01:31:36.120
Why did you value that feedback?

01:31:36.120 --> 01:31:38.040
How did that make you feel?

01:31:38.040 --> 01:31:41.120
Think of that as you start to craft and learn

01:31:41.120 --> 01:31:44.280
to give feedback more frequently to your team.

01:31:44.280 --> 01:31:45.600
And we have a framework.

01:31:45.600 --> 01:31:47.940
This is one of my favorite frameworks to use,

01:31:47.940 --> 01:31:50.240
which is SBI framework with the twist.

01:31:50.240 --> 01:31:52.360
Now we can divide this framework into three parts.

01:31:52.360 --> 01:31:55.320
Situation, behavior impact, and outcomes.

01:31:55.320 --> 01:31:59.760
Now, situation as in, what was the situation that happened

01:31:59.760 --> 01:32:01.420
where you felt the need to give feedback?

01:32:01.420 --> 01:32:02.880
What was the situation like?

01:32:02.880 --> 01:32:04.240
Were you in meetings?

01:32:04.240 --> 01:32:05.280
Did someone say something?

01:32:05.280 --> 01:32:06.960
What was the situation?

01:32:06.960 --> 01:32:11.760
What is the behavior that you would like to change?

01:32:11.760 --> 01:32:14.600
What behavior impacted the team or impacted you

01:32:14.600 --> 01:32:16.020
or someone else on your team?

01:32:16.020 --> 01:32:18.160
You wanna be very careful of that.

01:32:18.160 --> 01:32:20.560
For example, if they're interrupting in meetings,

01:32:20.560 --> 01:32:22.680
then you need them not to do that.

01:32:22.680 --> 01:32:25.720
So let them know the behavior needs to change

01:32:25.720 --> 01:32:28.540
where they need to stop interrupting others

01:32:28.540 --> 01:32:32.240
and make others feel excluded.

01:32:32.240 --> 01:32:37.240
And impact is that your other team members felt excluded

01:32:37.280 --> 01:32:40.000
and that is the impact from their behavior, right?

01:32:40.000 --> 01:32:42.040
And the situation was that you were in a team meeting,

01:32:42.040 --> 01:32:43.880
discussing ideas and thoughts,

01:32:43.880 --> 01:32:45.900
and someone came up really strongly

01:32:45.900 --> 01:32:48.960
and kept interrupting everyone else in the meeting.

01:32:48.960 --> 01:32:51.040
That's not how you want, right?

01:32:51.040 --> 01:32:53.800
And that's the essence of SBI framework.

01:32:53.800 --> 01:32:55.780
But I like to add a twist and say,

01:32:55.780 --> 01:32:57.760
let's also focus on the outcome

01:32:57.760 --> 01:33:00.840
because then that helps you with feedback in general.

01:33:00.840 --> 01:33:04.000
What is the outcome you would like from that conversation

01:33:04.000 --> 01:33:05.560
when you're giving feedback?

01:33:05.560 --> 01:33:09.640
Is the outcome that you would want their behavior to change,

01:33:09.640 --> 01:33:13.440
you don't want them to interrupt others in the meeting,

01:33:13.440 --> 01:33:14.840
then let them know that.

01:33:14.840 --> 01:33:17.680
So the outcome here, it's not just you and me, right?

01:33:17.680 --> 01:33:18.800
Let's start to figure out

01:33:18.800 --> 01:33:20.940
what is that behavior that we need to change

01:33:20.940 --> 01:33:23.680
because it's not them, it's the behavior, right?

01:33:23.680 --> 01:33:26.160
And you can also use this infographic here

01:33:26.160 --> 01:33:29.160
to use SBI framework with the twist

01:33:29.160 --> 01:33:31.760
so that you can easily reference it in the future

01:33:31.760 --> 01:33:33.160
when you're giving feedback.

01:33:35.120 --> 01:33:36.440
So let's say you have a situation,

01:33:36.440 --> 01:33:38.240
let's take a look at an example.

01:33:38.240 --> 01:33:40.640
You have a team member that is very senior in the org

01:33:40.640 --> 01:33:43.120
and is not mentoring junior devs

01:33:43.120 --> 01:33:45.100
and not really prioritizing them.

01:33:45.100 --> 01:33:47.080
What is something that you need to do?

01:33:47.080 --> 01:33:48.840
Well, your first instinct is going to be

01:33:48.840 --> 01:33:50.000
that this is not okay.

01:33:50.000 --> 01:33:51.960
This in this case would be

01:33:51.960 --> 01:33:55.560
that you would want this behavior to stop or change

01:33:55.560 --> 01:33:57.880
and that's going to be your first instinct.

01:33:57.880 --> 01:34:00.760
But the second is going to be that they're mean

01:34:00.760 --> 01:34:02.020
and they're rude,

01:34:02.020 --> 01:34:04.080
that they're constantly talking over people.

01:34:04.080 --> 01:34:06.200
This is not making the team feel good

01:34:06.200 --> 01:34:08.360
and the team is not speaking up because of that.

01:34:08.360 --> 01:34:09.560
And that is the impact.

01:34:10.800 --> 01:34:14.080
So let's try giving this feedback using this framework.

01:34:14.080 --> 01:34:16.120
And these are the list of questions that I have

01:34:16.120 --> 01:34:18.600
in the workbook that you can use

01:34:18.600 --> 01:34:20.800
to craft this specific feedback.

01:34:20.800 --> 01:34:22.320
That is, what is the outcome?

01:34:22.320 --> 01:34:23.400
What is the context?

01:34:23.400 --> 01:34:24.800
What is the situation?

01:34:24.800 --> 01:34:26.320
What behavior needs to change?

01:34:26.320 --> 01:34:27.800
Why do you think this happened?

01:34:27.800 --> 01:34:30.760
And what was the impact that it had on the team?

01:34:30.760 --> 01:34:32.080
You can use these questions

01:34:32.080 --> 01:34:34.320
to craft a really good feedback message.

01:34:34.320 --> 01:34:35.760
So workbook time.

01:34:35.760 --> 01:34:38.480
You want to collectively craft a feedback message

01:34:38.480 --> 01:34:41.140
using a SBI model with a twist

01:34:41.140 --> 01:34:44.600
and make sure that you give it a shot.

01:34:44.600 --> 01:34:46.000
So what are some important things

01:34:46.000 --> 01:34:47.880
to note about feedback, right?

01:34:47.880 --> 01:34:49.800
You always want to be open to hearing

01:34:49.800 --> 01:34:52.700
their side of the story, their feedback, right?

01:34:52.700 --> 01:34:56.120
Because as much as you are open to sharing yours,

01:34:56.120 --> 01:34:57.640
they also have a side to it,

01:34:57.640 --> 01:34:59.080
which is very important.

01:34:59.080 --> 01:35:00.800
So maybe you don't have context

01:35:00.800 --> 01:35:03.240
to new things that could have happened

01:35:03.240 --> 01:35:05.560
that maybe they did not let anyone else talk

01:35:05.560 --> 01:35:08.080
because you were in a client meeting

01:35:08.080 --> 01:35:11.320
and you knew a backstory

01:35:11.320 --> 01:35:14.280
which would significantly impact the project.

01:35:15.520 --> 01:35:17.320
There could be many different reasons.

01:35:17.320 --> 01:35:19.640
And then suddenly you start feeling like,

01:35:19.640 --> 01:35:22.160
oh, that's why they kept interrupting others.

01:35:22.160 --> 01:35:24.040
And even though that's not okay,

01:35:24.040 --> 01:35:25.640
and they should never do that,

01:35:25.640 --> 01:35:27.000
there was a better way to handle it.

01:35:27.000 --> 01:35:29.640
And that feedback can change based on that.

01:35:29.640 --> 01:35:32.080
And lastly, document the action items.

01:35:32.080 --> 01:35:33.360
What are the takeaways for them?

01:35:33.360 --> 01:35:37.240
And ask them what the action items were from this

01:35:37.240 --> 01:35:40.080
so that you can make sure that there's alignment.

01:35:40.080 --> 01:35:43.560
The answers should always align to what you talked about,

01:35:43.560 --> 01:35:46.280
but, and it shouldn't be perfect,

01:35:46.280 --> 01:35:49.320
but it should have the essence of what you are looking for.

